4997,Corruption Currents: Russian Hackers Targeted Orthodox Church Figures,"By Samuel Rubenfeld Samuel Rubenfeld The Wall Street Journal BiographySamuel Rubenfeld Aug 29, 2018 3:32 pm ET  A general view for the Kiev Pechersk Lavra, an Orthodox Christian monastery, through branches of trees covered by hoarfrost during a sunny day on Jan. 18, 2017, in Kiev, Ukraine. Photo: European Pressphoto Agency  A daily roundup of corruption news from across the web. We also provide a daily roundup of important risk & compliance stories via our daily newsletter, The Morning Risk Report, which readers can sign up for here. Follow us on Twitter at @WSJRisk.  Bribery:  Armenian police accused managers of a local unit of a French water company of bribery. The company expressed indignation over the accusations, the report said. (ARKA)  China will prosecute the former chairman of a state-owned conglomerate after an investigation found he took bribes. (Reuters)  Egyptian prosecutors are investigating officials for allegedly taking bribes from commodities firms. (ET)  A prison guard in New York pleaded guilty to taking bribes to smuggle mobile phones, alcohol and food to an inmate. That inmate was a billionaire Turkish gold trader then awaiting trial on sanctions-breaches charges; he later pleaded guilty. (AP)  Local cases: Russian school notebooks feature remarks from a mayor accused of bribery. The FBI is investigating whether a former Ohio House speaker took bribes. (RFE/RL, CE)  Cybercrime:  Russian hackers indicted by the U.S. special counsel have separately spent years trying to steal the private correspondence of senior Orthodox Christian figures. (AP)  The Bank of Spain's website was hit by a cyberattack. (Reuters)  U.K. data breach reports have soared under the new European rules. (FT)  Turning virtual currency into real cash is proving difficult for cybercriminals. Thai police are stepping up an investigation into such conduct. (BBC, AP)  Money Laundering:  Paul Manafort filed a motion to move his money laundering trial to Roanoke, Va., saying the jury pool in Washington, D.C. would be biased against him. Earlier, the trial was delayed by a week and special counsel Robert Mueller's team gave an update on the length of its case. His first trial showed the limits of encryption. (Bloomberg, Reuters, WP, Politico, Politico, WP, DB)  A former Barbadian minister pleaded not guilty in the U.S. to money-laundering charges stemming from allegations he accepted bribes from an insurance firm. He hired a former attorney general to help him locally. (Loop, CNS, NN)  The CEO of a Belgian trading firm stepped down in the wake of the Paradise Papers leak. (BT)  A Venezuelan TV mogul with a Miami mansion has emerged as a key suspect in a U.S. money-laundering investigation of PdVSA. The man's lawyer denied any wrongdoing by his client, who wasn't charged. (MH)  Malaysian police again questioned the former prime minister as investigations continue into alleged money laundering linked to 1MDB. Separately, authorities arrested a former corporate executive on money-laundering charges; the person didn't appear to have a response. (NST, FMT, NST, Star)  Thousands of Hong Kong bank accounts were used to launder about $500 million by fraudsters based locally and internationally. (SCMP)  Latvia's finance minister said the country's anti-money laundering office has been the country's weakest link in its fight against illicit finances. (LSM)  Estonia's government says it is stepping up its fight against money laundering. (ERR, BC)  A Singaporean bank turned to a local startup to start using machine learning to target money laundering. (BT, Edge, FIN)  Nigerian officials are investigating allegations of money laundering by a state government. (NN)  Indian authorities filed charges in a hotel-linked money laundering case. Authorities questioned a minister in a separate case; his son has been questioned twice. (NDTV, PTI, TOI, PTI, TN)  Mexico charged a former governor with money laundering. He is in custody after an extradition. (TeleSUR)  Deutsche Bank AG threatened to end its business with Russia's government if it didn't comply with know-your-customer rules. (Bloomberg)  A Vietnamese beauty salon operator was sentenced to prison for laundering $1.29 million in drug money. (NZH)  Sanctions:  China's Sinopec is wary of U.S. secondary sanctions that target businesses dealing with Iran. Meanwhile, Iranians are angry at government corruption as U.S. sanctions bite. (SCMP, FT)  Whistleblowers:  Goldman Sachs Group Inc. wants to use an arbitrator to resolve allegations raised by a whistleblower. (Bloomberg)  The number of calls to the U.K. tax-fraud hotline have doubled in the last year. (FT)  General Anticorruption:  A referendum in Colombia on seven anti-corruption measures failed to reach a quorum. Why did the referendum fail? (Reuters, NBC)  The U.S. Senate passed a bill requiring lobbyists to disclose when they've been convicted of a corruption-related crime. (WP)  Curbing corruption and recovering stolen state assets are Pakistan's biggest challenges, the new premier said. (Arya, ANI)  China's president is targeting more generals as his anti-corruption drive continues. (SCMP, Reuters)  Sri Lankan courts began investigating graft from the prior government. (AFP)  Kenya's embarking on another anti-corruption purge. (SM, Tuko, NN, CapitalFM)  Rep. Duncan Hunter (R., Calif.) said to his wife should be left alone days after he blamed her and they both pleaded not guilty to corruption charges. (HuffPost, NYT, CNN)  U.K. banks have paid 71 billion pounds in penalties since the financial crisis. (MS)",2018-08-29,eng,wsj.com,-0.2705882352941177,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/riskandcompliance/2018/08/29/corruption-currents-russian-hackers-targeted-orthodox-church-figures/,3.08
