n Carreyrou.  Malware campaign hits poorly secured websites. A months-long hacking campaign has managed to infiltrate thousands of websites running content management systems such as WordPress. Ars Technica reports that the hackers have managed to snare victims into installing malware and remote access trojans on their machines by targeting web site visitors with ""authentic-appearing messages"" instructing them to install browser updates.  Reddit targets Russians. The website Tuesday said it would remove more than 900 ""suspicious accounts"" it believes are linked to Russia's Internet Research Agency, a notorious troll farm, the FT reports. CEO Steve Huffman in a post said ""few"" of the accounts had a ""visible impact on the site.""  Investors want to know more about YouTube. A number of investors are asking Alphabet Inc. to share sales and profit information about its YouTube unit, arguing the video site has become a material part of the company's business but remains a financial enigma. The WSJ's Douglas MacMillan has more.  EPA assesses Twitter threats. The Environmental Protection Agency is examining tweets and posts on social media about its chief Scott Pruitt to justify his security staff of 2o people. The staff provides Mr. Pruitt 24-hour protection, even on trips to Disneyland. The New York Times reports that ""the social media efforts have come under scrutiny by some Democratic lawmakers, as well as senior officials at the E.P.A., who said the review had uncovered individuals sounding off against Mr. Pruitt but had found no actionable threats against him.""  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  President Donald Trump warned Russia that U.S. missiles ""will be coming"" to Syria. (WSJ)  The White House said President Trump believes he has the authority to fire special counsel Robert Mueller, as lawmakers from both parties warned against doing so one day after an FBI raid on his lawyer. (WSJ)  After climbing for much of the past two decades, Chinese investment into the U.S. dropped by 36% last year as Beijing imposed capital controls and Washington increased scrutiny of incoming deals. (WSJ)  A 21st Century Fox office in the U.K. was raided by the European Commission, which is conducting an antitrust probe into sports rights and distribution of sports content. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-04-11,eng,wsj.com,0.1215686274509804,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/04/11/the-morning-download-tyson-cto-rewiring-83-year-old-food-giant/,3.08
5032,The Morning Download: Salesforce President Explains Thought Behind MuleSoft Deal,"By Steve Rosenbush Steve Rosenbush The Wall Street Journal BiographySteve Rosenbush @Steve_Rosenbush ciojournal steven.rosenbush@wsj.com Mar 29, 2018 8:17 am ET  Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning. Subscribe to WSJ Pro Cybersecurity for in-depth coverage on cybersecurity trends, breaches and best practices.  Good morning, CIOs. Last week, Salesforce.com Inc. announced that it would pay $6.5 billion to acquire MuleSoft Inc., the largest deal in its 19-year history, and one that addresses the work of many CIOs. MuleSoft helps companies develop and deploy application programming interfaces used to connect one application to another. The API enables everything from the app store to the browser extension, or the banking tool that plugs into a messaging platform. Mulesoft has been focused on helping companies use APIs to break up their internal ""ball of mud"" and better connect with the external world, according to founder Ross Mason.  CIO Journal's Angus Loten spoke to Salesforce President and COO Keith Block about the strategy behind the deal: Says Mr. Block:  What makes APIs so valuable? ""As we were listening to CEOs, the whole notion of integration and data kept coming up over and over again. They are so frustrated that they can't unlock data from their legacy systems. That is the strategic nature of why we are acquiring MuleSoft.  The API and transition to the cloud. ""Over time all of these organizations' legacy systems will be moving to the cloud. But it has taken years to build up these systems and I don't think we can underestimate how much legacy is still out there.""  How do you make use of APIs in your business? Leave a comment and let us know.  SECURITY  A Boeing 737 MAX 7 taxis at Boeing Field, on March 16, 2018 in Seattle. Stephen Brashear/Getty Images  Boeing finds malware. The airplane maker said it ""detected a limited intrusion of malware"" that affected ""a small number of systems,"" Reuters reports. The Seattle Times Wednesday reported that the company was hit by the WannaCry computer virus, but that executives were offering assurances that the attack was quashed.  Atlanta hobbled by ransomware attack. One week after Atlanta was hit by a ransomware attack, citizens still can't pay traffic tickets or other city bills online and passengers searching the terminals of Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport for free Wi-Fi do so in vain. The New York Times reports that investigators are looking into the SamSam group, a ransomware player ""known for choosing targets that are the most likely to accede to its high ransom demands.""  PRIVACY  MLADEN ANTONOV  Facebook limiting information shared with Data brokers. The measures affect a group of so-called data brokers such as Acxiom Corp. and Oracle Corp.'s Oracle Data Cloud, formerly known as DataLogix, that gather shopping and other information on consumers that Facebook for years has incorporated into the ad-targeting system that is at the core of its business. The Journal reports that Facebook Inc. is making the changes as part of a broader internal review of how it handles user information. Bloomberg has a slightly different take on Facebook's privacy moves: ""The reality is that most, if not all, of the measures are required by the General Data Protection Regulation, a law imposed by the European Union which becomes enforceable from May 25.""  Apple's Tim Cook calls for privacy regulation. Apple Inc.'s CEO Wednesday said consumers should have more visibility into not only what personal information they share online but how companies stitch together that information to better understand their users, the WSJ's Tripp Mickle reports. ""To me, it's creepy when I look at something and all of a sudden it's chasing me all across the web,"" he said.  Playboy deactivates Facebook accounts. The magazine said Wednesday that ""the recent news about Facebook's alleged mismanagement of users' data has solidified our decision to suspend our activity on the platform at this time.""  LEADERSHIP  Credit-reporting firm Equifax Inc. appointed Mark Begor, a former General Electric Co. executive, as its new chief executive on Wednesday. MARC BRYAN-BROWN  Equifax taps former GE executive as CEO. Equifax Inc. has appointed former General Electric Co. executive Mark Begor as chief executive, the second change to the position since the credit-reporting company disclosed a massive cyberattack in September. The Journal's AnnaMaria Andriotis has more.  Uber's head of freight leaves company. Uber Technologies Inc.'s head of freight trucking, Lior Ron, is leaving the company, a prominent departure in the wake of a controversial crash involving one of its self-driving vehicles, the Journal's Greg Bensinger reports. Mr. Ron co-founded self-driving truck startup Otto, and was a figure in the recent trial concerning Uber's acquisition of the startup, which Alphabet Inc.'s Waymo alleged was used as a means to obtain self-driving vehicle trade secrets. Uber denied the claims.  Docker founder to leave. Solomon Hykes, who helped make containters the hottest technology for the enterprise, is leaving the company, Geekwire reports. In a blog post Mr. Hykes, who served as Docker's CTO, said the company needs a new technology chief ""with decades of experience shipping and supporting software for the largest corporations in the world.""  HP Inc. COO dies. Jonathan Flaxman, 61, served at HP for more than 36 years, Reuters reports.  MORE TECHNOLOGY NEWS  Jeff Bezos, founder and chief executive officer of Amazon.com Inc. Mike Kane/Bloomberg News  Amazon shares slide on Trump news. The stock decline Wednesday came after Axios reported President Donald Trump is ""obsessed with Amazon"" and has discussed changing Amazon's tax treatment due to concerns about how the company is impacting mom-and-pop businesses. Axios also reported that Mr. Trump has questioned ""if there may be any way to go after Amazon with antitrust or competition law."" The WSJ's Chelsey Dulaney has more.  Treasury to use national security laws to shield U.S. tech from China. The U.S. Treasury Department is planning to use laws designed to address national emergencies to block Chinese firms from acquiring advanced U.S. technology. An administration official tells the WSJ's Bob Davis that the move is part of a Trump administration initiative to thwart what it sees as Beijing's efforts to give itself an economic and military edge. China's licensing requirements, subsidized financing, involvement of state-owned firms and pressure on U.S. firms to transfer technology are all increasingly harming U.S. technology firms, the official said.  Bosses are using AI on employees. Some companies are turning to software to sniff out differences between what employees say and how they feel and regulators are struggling to keep up, the WSJ's Imani Moise reports. A number of software companies including HireVue Inc. and Syndio offer artificial-intelligence tools to help make decisions about hiring, firing and compensation. That worries employees who are wary of being psychoanalyzed by software, and some employment lawyers fret that AI programs might contain biases that could lead to workplace discrimination.  BMW, Daimler team up on tech. German automakers Daimler AG and BMW AG, fierce rivals in the luxury-car business, are joining forces to try to fend off competition from tech giants to their on-demand transportation services, the WSJ's William Boston reports. The German auto makers said Wednesday they will form a joint-venture company that combines Daimler's Car2Go and BMW's DriveNow car-sharing providers, as well as ride-hailing services such as Daimler's Moovel and MyTaxi and others.  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  President Donald Trump on Wednesday said he had ousted his Veterans Affairs secretary, David Shulkin, and tapped his personal White House physician, Ronny Jackson, as his replacement. (WSJ)  The percentage of individuals receiving tax audits declined for the sixth consecutive year in 2017 to reach the lowest level since 2002, further showing the effect of budget cuts at the Internal Revenue Service. (WSJ)  For the first time, students in more than half of all U.S. states are paying more in tuition to attend public colleges or universities than the government contributes. (WSJ)  The Trump administration has more than tripled the money flowing from an Obama-era transportation program to projects in rural areas. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-03-29,eng,wsj.com,0.1529411764705881,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/03/29/the-morning-download-salesforce-president-explains-thought-behind-mulesoft-deal/,3.08
5033,The Morning Ledger: China Slams Trump's Tariffs as Allies Hope for Talks,"By Tatyana Shumsky Tatyana Shumsky The Wall Street Journal BiographyTatyana Shumsky @MissShumsky Tatyana.Shumsky@wsj.com Mar 9, 2018 7:13 am ET  Sign up for the Morning Ledger, emailed to you each weekday morning. Follow us on Twitter: @CFOJournal. All CFO Journal-produced content can be accessed without a subscription.  In this April 25, 2016 photo, a man works in a steel market in Yichang in central China's Hubei province. AP  Good morning. China criticized new steel and aluminum tariffs announced by President Donald Trump on Thursday, while several U.S. allies reacted more cautiously, embracing what the White House promised would be some flexibility in implementation, report the WSJ's Wayne Ma, William Horobin and Doug Cameron.  China -- the main target of Washington's move to introduce tariffs of 25% on imported steel and 10% on aluminum -- responded strongly Friday. The country's commerce ministry said it would ""take effective measures to protect China's rights."" Chinese metal-industry trade groups called on Beijing to retaliate against U.S. imports; from stainless steel and electronics to coal and farm products.  The U.S. excluded Canada and Mexico, contingent on negotiations over the North American Free Trade Agreement. Washington said other allies might win exemptions depending on negotiations in coming weeks and months. Many American allies, including South Korea, Japan and the European Union, lobbied hard to avoid the measures from the start. While some expressed disappointment Friday at not being exempted, they said they would take Washington up on its offer to negotiate.  THE DAY AHEAD  The U.S. Labor Department will release its February non-farm payrolls report at 8.30 a.m. ET. Economists expect employers added 205,000 jobs during the month and see the unemployment rate ticking down to 4.0%. Here are five things to watch.  Revlon Inc. is set to release earnings today.  CFO JOURNAL EXCLUSIVE  The logo of German pharmaceuticals company Merck is seen in Darmstadt, Germany, March 7, 2018. Merck  Merck doubles down on debt reduction. Merck KGaA could forego mergers and acquisitions in 2018 and 2019 in a bid to reduce debt, the finance chief of the German drug maker said.  ""We will show restraint,"" Chief Financial Officer Marcus Kuhnert told CFO Journal's Nina Trentmann. The company budgeted a maximum of €500 million ($615.7 million) on deals this year, Mr. Kuhnert said.  Merck spent $17 billion to buy laboratory equipment maker Sigma-Aldrich Corp. in 2015, completing the largest deal in its history. The company used cash on hand, sold €7 billion in bonds and took on €4 billion in bank loans to fund the transaction. The deal added €11 billion to Merck's debt load.  CORPORATE NEWS  Disney Chairman and Chief Executive Officer Bob Iger in Hollywood, Calif., U.S., Jan. 22, 2018. EPA/Shutterstock  Disney shareholders don't back pay plan for CEO. Walt Disney Co. shareholders voted down a nonbinding endorsement of the compensation given to Chairman and Chief Executive Robert Iger following an increase in December, the first time a majority of votes were cast against such a proposal at the company.  Boeing has 'cash horsepower' for acquisitions. Boeing Co. can absorb transactions on the scale of a proposed tie-up with Brazil's Embraer SA without putting at risk internal investments in its business or plans to return cash to shareholders, chief executive Dennis Muilenburg told Reuters.  Tesla's Musk tells Trump China trade rules make things difficult. Tesla Inc.'s Chief Executive Elon Musk took to Twitter on Thursday to lobby the U.S. president on China's trade stance on auto makers, saying the Middle Kingdom's current rules ""make things very difficult."" Meanwhile, shareholder advisory group Institutional Shareholder Services said it opposes Mr. Musk's compensation package, two weeks before shareholders are due to vote on the proposal, reports the Financial Times.  Uber to exit Southeast Asia for stake in rival. Uber Technologies Inc. has reached an agreement in principle to sell most of its Southeast Asia operations to local rival Grab Inc., ending a costly fight for market share in the fast-growing region, according to people familiar with the matter.  Cigna deal shows being a health insurer isn't enough anymore. Cigna Corp.'s $54 billion deal for Express Scripts Holding Co. is the latest sign that health care's biggest players believe they can no longer go it alone, and they must branch into other businesses to forge integrated products aimed at curbing costs.  J&J says discounts cut prices for its drugs. Johnson & Johnson said the average price paid for its medicines in the U.S. fell by 4.6% last year due to the company's discounts, though the company's pharmaceutical sales still rose 6.7% to $21.5 billion during the period.  Toys ""R"" Us weighs shutting all U.S. stores. Troubled toy chain Toys ""R"" Us Inc. is preparing to liquidate all of its U.S. stores and abandon efforts to restructure through the bankruptcy process, people familiar with the matter said, after a weak holiday season torpedoed plans to reorganize.  Hess wins peace with activist investor. Hess Corp. said it would buy back an additional $1 billion in shares, heading off a potentially nasty proxy fight with activist hedge fund Elliott Management Corp.  Wynn Resorts to settle litigation with Universal Entertainment. Wynn Resorts Ltd. will pay $2.6 billion to settle litigation with Universal Entertainment Corp., which at one time held a 20% stake in the casino giant but was forced by Wynn in 2012 to give up its shares.  Claire's plans bankruptcy. Fashion accessories chain Claire's Stores Inc. is preparing to file for bankruptcy in the coming weeks and is closing in on a deal to pass control to lenders including Elliott Capital Management and Monarch Alternative Capital LP, reports Bloomberg.  REGULATION  GM Chief Executive Mary Barra is seen in Houston, Texas, U.S., March 7, 2018. Bloomberg News  GM faces renewed prospect of $1 billion payout over ignition safety. General Motors Co. again faces the prospect of a potential $1 billion stock payout to address claims stemming from the auto giant's ignition-switch crisis, after a trust for the company's bankruptcy estate renewed discussions with plaintiffs about a settlement.  Merrill Lynch settles SEC claims. Bank of America Corp.'s Merrill Lynch unit agreed Thursday to pay $1.4 million to settle U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission allegations that it didn't do enough to investigate red flags at a Chinese company whose unregistered securities it sold.  Soured Lehman mortgage claims valued at $2.4 billion. Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc. caused $2.4 billion in damages to investors holding securities backed by shaky home mortgages, a New York bankruptcy judge ruled Thursday, ending one of the last remaining disputes in the defunct bank's nearly decadelong liquidation.  FDA approves 23andMe kit, reverses course. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration this week allowed a genetic testing firm to sell kits to consumers to test whether they carry gene mutations that put them at higher risk for breast and ovarian cancer. The action, part of a broader regulatory shift, is the first time the Food and Drug Administration has allowed a company -- in this case 23andMe Inc. -- to market such a cancer-risk test directly to the public.  China fast-tracks IPO approval for Foxconn unit. Foxconn Industrial Internet Co., a fledgling unit of Taiwanese electronics manufacturer Foxconn Technology Group, jumped to the front of the queue of Chinese companies waiting to go public, showing how regulators are moving quickly to allow select technology stalwarts to list at home.  ECONOMY  In this Feb. 23, 2018, photo a for sale sign stands in front of a house, in Walpole, Mass., U.S. AP  Mortgage rates at four-year high threaten to cool housing. U.S. mortgage rates have hit their highest level since 2014, a new challenge for a housing market that has been central to the economic recovery but remains vulnerable to even modest headwinds.  U.S. household net worth climbs further into record territory. Americans' wealth hit nearly $100 trillion in the final quarter of last year on the back of rising stock markets and property prices.  ECB prepares to wind down bond buying program. The European Central Bank on Thursday took another step toward ending its bond buying program, currently scheduled to run until September, by dropping a long-held pledge to accelerate the €30 billion-a-month program if the region's economy deteriorates.  CFO MOVES  Walgreens Boots Alliance Inc., a Deerfield, Ill. pharmacy chain operator, named James Kehoe chief financial officer, effective June 1. He will suceed the company's current CFO George Fairweather who will become a senior advisor to Chief Executive Officer Stefano Pessina, effective June 1.  Mr. Kehoe joins Walgreens from Takeda Pharmaceutical Co. where has served as CFO and corporate officer since June 2016. He will receive a base salary of $900,000, and his target annual cash incentive opportunity will be 125% of his base salary. Mr. Kehoe also will be eligible to participate in the company's stock-based incentive program providing for annual stock option and performance share grants, with the total combined target grant date award value for this position currently being $3.5 million. He will also receive a sign on bonus of $2.5 million that will vest over time and a new hire restricted stock grant valued at $4.5 million, according to a filing.  THE WEEKEND READER  Every weekend we select a handful of in-depth articles we think are worth a bit of your time, either because they peel back the layers on a compelling business story, or somehow make us look at business in a different light.  America is giving away the $30 billion medical marijuana industry. The medical weed business is booming in Israel and Canada, where startups are driving innovation. In the U.S., by contrast, development has been stymied by regulators, and the economy is missing out, writes Bloomberg Businessweek.  A close look at a 'hacker hero.' Marcus Hutchins, a 23-year old cybersecurity researcher, helped stop the WannaCry ransomware attack last May. Three months later, the FBI arrested him for allegedly creating banking malware. ""I think everyone can see I have some shady things in my past,"" Mr. Hutchins tells New York Magazine.  Why companies shouldn't try to avoid politics. Companies used to avoid political issues at almost any cost. But sometimes that strategy leads to more harm than good, researchers found, reports Harvard Business Review.  The Morning Ledger from CFO Journal cues up the most important news in corporate finance every weekday morning. Nina Trentmann contributed to today's Ledger. Send tips, suggestions and complaints to the editor: kimberly.johnson@wsj.com.",2018-03-09,eng,wsj.com,0.2392156862745098,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cfo/2018/03/09/the-morning-ledger-march-9/,3.08
5034,Due Diligence on Cybersecurity Becomes Bigger Factor in M&A,"Close scrutiny of tech operations can uncover cybersecurity gaps before deals close By Kim S. Nash and Ezequiel Minaya Ezequiel Minaya The Wall Street Journal BiographyEzequiel Minaya @zekeminaya #!/zeke.minaya ezequiel.minaya@wsj.com March 5, 2018 12:01 a.m. ET  Automatic Data Processing Inc. ADP -0.05% deployed a team of cybersecurity, risk management and financial-crime specialists to WorkMarket before acquiring it in January.  The ADP team combed the software maker's technology, practices and internal policies. It also interviewed staff about monitoring for intrusions, training employees and performing other security tasks. The payroll processor also hired a cybersecurity firm to do its own evaluation.  Security problems, said ADP's chief security officer Roland Cloutier, could kill any deal.  ""If we found out data was exfiltrated, we may walk away,"" he said. ""We've looked at a lot of companies and only purchased a few. Security always plays a part.""  Companies are intensifying due diligence of acquisition targets to avoid costly cybersecurity surprises, particularly when intellectual property, such as software code or customer data drive the deal.  Scrutiny will continue as merger and acquisition activity heats up on expectations of extra cash from lower corporate tax rates. As of late February, 18 transactions valued at more than $5 billion each have been announced -- up from 10 such big deals during the same period in each of 2017 and 2016, according to Dealogic.  Gaps in data protection, undiscovered breaches, regulatory violations and other holes in a company's technology operations can threaten transactions. Such problems can also decrease the value of a deal or leave an acquirer liable for problems after a merger.  Read more CIO Journal coverage here:  CIO Network 2018 5 Things to Do in Cybersecurity Due Diligence The Inside Story of When Verizon Learned of the Yahoo Breach  ADP investigators typically look for troublespots such as signs of an unauthorized presence on the target's network and scant or no evidence that employees have received security training.  No significant problems surfaced at WorkMarket, but deep study of a target's cybersecurity helps executives forecast deal costs, Mr. Cloutier said. ADP typically spends two to four months on the process.  Problems can arise even years later. FedEx Corp. FDX -0.11% moved quickly last month to secure a server that exposed data from customer driver's licenses and passports. FedEx inherited the server when it bought e-commerce service Bongo International in 2014.  Four or five years ago, cybersecurity due diligence consisted of asking a few questions in a short phone call, said Evan Wolff, a partner at Crowell & Moring LLP.  Now data compromises can diminish the value of a transaction, he said. Suspected theft of sensitive data uncovered through due diligence ""becomes a business issue,"" he said.  Verizon Communications Inc. VZ -0.20% last year renegotiated an acquisition proposal with Yahoo Inc.'s AABA -2.49% board after details emerged about massive hacking incidents. Verizon would ultimately learn all three billion Yahoo accounts were hit.  As a result, Verizon lowered it's proposed purchase price by $350 million to $4.48 billion.  The company did studies to assess potential reputational harm and future risks, said Craig Silliman, Verizon's general counsel, speaking at a Wall Street Journal conference in December. ""We said, 'We feel like we have enough clarity that we can put parameters around the risk here and negotiate a deal that effectively compensates us for the risk.'""  Home Depot Inc. HD -0.06% performed cyberrisk due diligence before buying retailer The Company Store and tool-rental firm Compact Power Equipment Inc. in 2017, said finance chief Carol Tomé.  ""Our plans are basically to integrate these companies,"" Ms. Tomé said. Their operations will be moved to Home Depot's platforms and networks, she said. ""So we're closing down any little holes that the threat actor could take advantage of.""  The company has assessed cyberrisk on potential deals for the past decade, according to a spokesman. Getting breached in 2014 elevated cybersecurity concerns among senior leaders at Home Depot, Ms. Tomé said. Hackers stole email and payment-card information of up to 56 million customers.  Home Depot's due diligence playbook includes penetration testing, Ms. Tomé said. ""We have a heightened sense of awareness in this area and our due diligence is exhaustive.""  Waste Management Inc. WM 1.87% doesn't dedicate a team to cyber issues during the diligence phase. The company instead focuses on the later stage of moving data from the target's systems into its own, said CFO Devina Rankin.  The company spends $100 million to $200 million a year on garbage and recycling haulers. Legal, finance and digital groups move data about employees at acquired companies, usually within a week of closing the transaction. Customer data is absorbed within one month, she said.  Acquirers sometimes find costly cybersecurity issues embedded in contracts that a target signed with its own customers, said Buck De Wolf, general counsel for General Electric Co.'s global research group. GE has purchased at least 14 companies since 2015, including several small software providers, according to its annual reports.  Small companies hungry for sales might make onerous promises about how they will help and what they will pay for in a data breach related to their products, Mr. De Wolf said, speaking at security conference in December. It can be ""a Trojan Horse"" when taking on a new company, he said. Reviewing contracts helps GE avoid these problems, he said.  -- Tatyana Shumsky contributed to this article.  Write to Ezequiel Minaya at ezequiel.minaya@wsj.com",2018-03-05,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/companies-sharpen-cyber-due-diligence-as-m-a-activity-revs-up-1520226061,3.08
5035,Survey Roundup: Economic Crime Claims More Victims,"By Ben DiPietro Ben DiPietro The Wall Street Journal BiographyBen DiPietro Mar 1, 2018 5:49 pm ET  A look at some recent surveys and reports dealing with risk and compliance issues. Send links of surveys and reports to ben.dipietro@wsj.com.  Crime Costs: An analysis of data from 7,228 organizations in 123 countries by PwC found 49% were a victim of economic crime in the past two years, up from 36% who said so in 2016. For the first time, more than half of the U.S. organizations (53%) said they were victimized; 38% said the same two years ago. Among U.S. organizations, 37% said the cost of the crime against them exceeded $1 million, compared to 18% of global responses. Fifty-two percent of U.S. organizations said they increased or significantly increased spending to fight fraud; 42% said they plan to.  ""Damage from economic crime goes far beyond financial loss,"" the PwC report said. ""Today, there's amplified danger of damage to a company's reputation. With public expectations of companies and their business partners at an all-time high, even a small matter can blow up into a large and consequential issue in the public eye.""  Culture of Morality: A survey of around 500 employees by ethics and compliance services firm LRN found 59% said their organizations would more successfully address their largest challenges if leadership had more moral authority, while 83% said their organization would make better decisions if it followed the Golden Rule and treated others as it wants to be treated.  Response Ability: A survey of around 1,300 senior executives by insurance brokerage Marsh found 19% said they are highly confident their organization can mitigate and respond to a cyber event, while 30% said their company has a plan to respond to cyberattacks.  Deadline Pressure: A survey of around 200 senior operations and security professionals by security firm Threat Stack found 52% said their company pares back security measures to meet a business deadline.  Banking on Fraud: A survey of 185 risk and fraud executives in the financial services sector by LexisNexis Risk Solutions found 62% of fraud losses at banks with more than $50 million in revenue involved identity fraud; 75% of respondents said verifying identities online is a top challenge.  Same Same: A survey of 1,301 IT security decision makers by security firm CyberArk found 46% said they rarely change their security strategy--even after a cyberattack.  Review Time: A survey of 624 executives by Experian Data Breach Resolution and Ponemon Institute found 88% said their organization has a data breach response plan but 66% said they have no scheduled time to review them or update them.  Incidental Impact: Cyber risk insurer Beazley analyzed about 2,600 data breaches in 2017 and found 36% of such incidents happened because of a hack or malware, 28% by accidental disclosure.  Technical Difficulties: A survey of around 800 enterprise executives comprised of chief executives, technical officers and chief financial officers by security firm Centrify and Dow Jones Customer Intelligence found 62% of CEOs named malware as their company's top cybersecurity threat, while 35% of technical officers did. Dow Jones Customer Intelligence is part of News Corp., which owns The Wall Street Journal.  Write to Ben DiPietro at ben.dipietro@wsj.com, and follow him on Twitter @BenDiPietro1.",2018-03-01,eng,wsj.com,-0.3254901960784313,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/riskandcompliance/2018/03/01/survey-roundup-economic-crime-claims-more-victims-higher-costs/,3.08
5036,The Morning Download: AI Researchers Look at the Negative Side,"By Steve Rosenbush Steve Rosenbush The Wall Street Journal BiographySteve Rosenbush @Steve_Rosenbush ciojournal steven.rosenbush@wsj.com Feb 21, 2018 8:15 am ET  The Shadow Dexterous robotic hand, manufactured by The Shadow Robot Company, touches the keyboard of an Apple Inc. MacBook Pro computer in London, Feb. 14, 2018. Photo: Luke MacGregor/Bloomberg Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning. Subscribe to WSJ Pro Cybersecurity for in-depth coverage on cybersecurity trends, breaches and best practices.  Good morning, CIOs. Somewhere between the happy and hopeful view that artificial intelligence will trigger a massive advance in the quality of human life, and the grim prediction that it will bring about humanity's demise, lies a vast expanse of gray, a field of restrained hope tempered by caution. A group of academic researchers has issued a new report that falls into this space. Says Reuters: ""The study, published on Wednesday by 25 technical and public policy researchers from Cambridge, Oxford and Yale universities along with privacy and military experts, sounded the alarm for the potential misuse of AI by rogue states, criminals and lone-wolf attackers.""  Mind the gap. ""We all agree there are a lot of positive applications of AI,"" Miles Brundage, a research fellow at Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute, explains in the Reuters story. ""There was a gap in the literature around the issue of malicious use.""  Deepfake news. ""Late last year, so-called 'deepfake' pornographic videos began to surface online, with celebrity faces realistically melded to different bodies. 'It happened in the regime of pornography rather than propaganda,' said Jack Clark, head of policy at OpenAI, the group founded by Tesla Inc. CEO Elon Musk and Silicon Valley investor Sam Altman ... 'But nothing about deepfakes suggests it can't be applied to propaganda.' ""  Q&A: Taking ClubCorp from filing cabinets to the cloud. ClubCorp Holdings Inc. CIO Patrick Benson talked with CIO Journal's Angus Loten about the challenges of digitizing a 60-year-old company. ""My advice is, if you're starting to migrate to the cloud, or your even thinking about it, start modifying your process today to mirror the process of what the future will look like as closely as possible. Any of your IT vendors will help you understand these processes. Don't wait. People's minds need to get rewired to the way things are going to work.""  SECURITY AND PRIVACY  Vehicles drive past the wall and towers of the Kremlin in central Moscow, Russia November 29, 2017. Maxim Shemetov / Reuters  Russian trolls tweeted disinformation long before U.S. election. An analysis of 221,641 tweets shows Russian trolls tried to incite chaos, fear and outrage about fake events before their 2016 election activity, as if they were testing to see how much they could get Americans to believe. The Wall Street Journal has the story.  Tesla cloud account hacked. Cybersecurity firm RedLock said hackers accessed Tesla Inc. Kubernetes consoles on Amazon Web Services to install cryptocurrency mining software. RedLock says the exposed areas held Tesla telemetry, mapping and services data.  Sessions forms cyber task force. The Tuesday announcement by the U.S. Attorney General on the creation of a Justice Department Cyber-Digital task force aimed in part at combating threats to election system met skepticism from some experts. ""This step basically takes a number of really complicated parallel issues in 'hard' cybersecurity and 'soft' information security and throws them into the same amorphous task force,"" a cybersecurity aide in the Obama administration, tells Reuters. The task force also will look at encryption, online terrorism and threats to infrastructure.  In strategic shift, North Korean hacking unit targets overseas. The group, referred to as ""Reaper"" by cybersecurity firm FireEye Inc., had long focused on harassing South Korea's public institutions, military and private companies. But last year, the unit attacked Japan, Vietnam and the Middle East, the WSJ's Timothy W. Martin reports. The Reaper group's emergence means two major North Korean cyber units have been unleashed to make global attacks. The other, often called ""Lazarus,"" has been linked to last year's WannaCry ransomware attack and the 2014 Sony Pictures hack.  MORE TECHNOLOGY NEWS  Pure refined cobalt in a Cobalt 27 warehouse in Rotterdam Herman van Heusden for The Wall Street Journal  Apple in talks to buy cobalt directly from miners. Apple is one of the largest end users of cobalt, a key ingredient of lithium-ion batteries, and a hot commodity among electric car makers, Bloomberg reports. The price of the metal, found in the Democratic Republic of Congo, has more than tripled in the past 18 months to trade at more than $80,000 a metric ton, Bloomberg says.  FCC takes another step on ending net neutrality. The Federal Communications Commission Thursday will publish its order ending net neutrality, Reuters reports. The FCC in December voted 3-2 in favor of the proposal. Congressional aides tell Reuters that following publication Congress will have 60 legislative days to vote on whether to overturn the FCC decision.  Walmart endures web-shift stumble. Walmart Inc. reported strong holiday sales but said online sales growth slowed during the quarter, marking a turn from three quarters of booming web growth, the WSJ's Sarah Nassauer reports.  Mining for the masses. The dramatic surge in bitcoin's price is luring individuals and corporations to try their hand at mining cryptocurrencies, giving rise to firms offering services that make mining easier for the masses by providing ready infrastructure, security and electricity. The Journal's Stephanie Yang has the story.  Trump administration's deregulation push heads for outer space. Vice President Mike Pence on Wednesday is expected to announce new moves promoting private ventures in space, including easing launch rules and putting the U.S. Commerce Department in charge of broader deregulation initiatives. The recommendations are intended to roll back regulations and better coordinate efforts to spur commercial space projects, the WSJ's Andy Pasztor reports.  Racist files suit against Twitter. Jared Taylor, a prominent white nationalist sued Twitter Inc. for kicking him off the social network. The WSJ's Georgia Wells reports that the action is latest in a spate of legal attacks by members of the far-right groups claiming tech companies discriminate against their viewpoints and challenging the idea that they operate neutral platforms.  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  3M Co. will pay $850 million to settle Minnesota's lawsuit, claiming the manufacturer contaminated water in the state for at least five decades. (WSJ)  A federal judge ruled AT&T isn't entitled to details of the Trump administration's internal discussions on the company's proposed merger with Time Warner. (WSJ)  The minutes of the Federal Reserves January meeting could reveal the expectations of officials over the $1.5 trillion tax cut package and how that might alter their plans to raise interest rates. (WSJ)  Government mandates requiring utilities to procure renewable energy should keep U.S. solar power growing, despite new Trump administration tariffs on imported solar panels that are poised to raise prices. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-02-21,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/02/21/the-morning-download-ai-researchers-look-at-the-negative-side/,3.08
5037,The Morning Download: AI Researchers Look at the Negative Side,"By Steve Rosenbush Steve Rosenbush The Wall Street Journal BiographySteve Rosenbush @Steve_Rosenbush ciojournal steven.rosenbush@wsj.com Feb 21, 2018 8:15 am ET  The Shadow Dexterous robotic hand, manufactured by The Shadow Robot Company, touches the keyboard of an Apple Inc. MacBook Pro computer in London, Feb. 14, 2018. Photo: Luke MacGregor/Bloomberg Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning. Subscribe to WSJ Pro Cybersecurity for in-depth coverage on cybersecurity trends, breaches and best practices.  Good morning, CIOs. Somewhere between the happy and hopeful view that artificial intelligence will trigger a massive advance in the quality of human life, and the grim prediction that it will bring about humanity's demise, lies a vast expanse of gray, a field of restrained hope tempered by caution. A group of academic researchers has issued a new report that falls into this space. Says Reuters: ""The study, published on Wednesday by 25 technical and public policy researchers from Cambridge, Oxford and Yale universities along with privacy and military experts, sounded the alarm for the potential misuse of AI by rogue states, criminals and lone-wolf attackers.""  Mind the gap. ""We all agree there are a lot of positive applications of AI,"" Miles Brundage, a research fellow at Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute, explains in the Reuters story. ""There was a gap in the literature around the issue of malicious use.""  Deepfake news. ""Late last year, so-called 'deepfake' pornographic videos began to surface online, with celebrity faces realistically melded to different bodies. 'It happened in the regime of pornography rather than propaganda,' said Jack Clark, head of policy at OpenAI, the group founded by Tesla Inc. CEO Elon Musk and Silicon Valley investor Sam Altman ... 'But nothing about deepfakes suggests it can't be applied to propaganda.' ""  Q&A: Taking ClubCorp from filing cabinets to the cloud. ClubCorp Holdings Inc. CIO Patrick Benson talked with CIO Journal's Angus Loten about the challenges of digitizing a 60-year-old company. ""My advice is, if you're starting to migrate to the cloud, or your even thinking about it, start modifying your process today to mirror the process of what the future will look like as closely as possible. Any of your IT vendors will help you understand these processes. Don't wait. People's minds need to get rewired to the way things are going to work.""  SECURITY AND PRIVACY  Vehicles drive past the wall and towers of the Kremlin in central Moscow, Russia November 29, 2017. Maxim Shemetov / Reuters  Russian trolls tweeted disinformation long before U.S. election. An analysis of 221,641 tweets shows Russian trolls tried to incite chaos, fear and outrage about fake events before their 2016 election activity, as if they were testing to see how much they could get Americans to believe. The Wall Street Journal has the story.  Tesla cloud account hacked. Cybersecurity firm RedLock said hackers accessed Tesla Inc. Kubernetes consoles on Amazon Web Services to install cryptocurrency mining software. RedLock says the exposed areas held Tesla telemetry, mapping and services data.  Sessions forms cyber task force. The Tuesday announcement by the U.S. Attorney General on the creation of a Justice Department Cyber-Digital task force aimed in part at combating threats to election system met skepticism from some experts. ""This step basically takes a number of really complicated parallel issues in 'hard' cybersecurity and 'soft' information security and throws them into the same amorphous task force,"" a cybersecurity aide in the Obama administration, tells Reuters. The task force also will look at encryption, online terrorism and threats to infrastructure.  In strategic shift, North Korean hacking unit targets overseas. The group, referred to as ""Reaper"" by cybersecurity firm FireEye Inc., had long focused on harassing South Korea's public institutions, military and private companies. But last year, the unit attacked Japan, Vietnam and the Middle East, the WSJ's Timothy W. Martin reports. The Reaper group's emergence means two major North Korean cyber units have been unleashed to make global attacks. The other, often called ""Lazarus,"" has been linked to last year's WannaCry ransomware attack and the 2014 Sony Pictures hack.  MORE TECHNOLOGY NEWS  Pure refined cobalt in a Cobalt 27 warehouse in Rotterdam Herman van Heusden for The Wall Street Journal  Apple in talks to buy cobalt directly from miners. Apple is one of the largest end users of cobalt, a key ingredient of lithium-ion batteries, and a hot commodity among electric car makers, Bloomberg reports. The price of the metal, found in the Democratic Republic of Congo, has more than tripled in the past 18 months to trade at more than $80,000 a metric ton, Bloomberg says.  FCC takes another step on ending net neutrality. The Federal Communications Commission Thursday will publish its order ending net neutrality, Reuters reports. The FCC in December voted 3-2 in favor of the proposal. Congressional aides tell Reuters that following publication Congress will have 60 legislative days to vote on whether to overturn the FCC decision.  Walmart endures web-shift stumble. Walmart Inc. reported strong holiday sales but said online sales growth slowed during the quarter, marking a turn from three quarters of booming web growth, the WSJ's Sarah Nassauer reports.  Mining for the masses. The dramatic surge in bitcoin's price is luring individuals and corporations to try their hand at mining cryptocurrencies, giving rise to firms offering services that make mining easier for the masses by providing ready infrastructure, security and electricity. The Journal's Stephanie Yang has the story.  Trump administration's deregulation push heads for outer space. Vice President Mike Pence on Wednesday is expected to announce new moves promoting private ventures in space, including easing launch rules and putting the U.S. Commerce Department in charge of broader deregulation initiatives. The recommendations are intended to roll back regulations and better coordinate efforts to spur commercial space projects, the WSJ's Andy Pasztor reports.  Racist files suit against Twitter. Jared Taylor, a prominent white nationalist sued Twitter Inc. for kicking him off the social network. The WSJ's Georgia Wells reports that the action is latest in a spate of legal attacks by members of the far-right groups claiming tech companies discriminate against their viewpoints and challenging the idea that they operate neutral platforms.  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  3M Co. will pay $850 million to settle Minnesota's lawsuit, claiming the manufacturer contaminated water in the state for at least five decades. (WSJ)  A federal judge ruled AT&T isn't entitled to details of the Trump administration's internal discussions on the company's proposed merger with Time Warner. (WSJ)  The minutes of the Federal Reserves January meeting could reveal the expectations of officials over the $1.5 trillion tax cut package and how that might alter their plans to raise interest rates. (WSJ)  Government mandates requiring utilities to procure renewable energy should keep U.S. solar power growing, despite new Trump administration tariffs on imported solar panels that are poised to raise prices. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-02-21,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,http://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/02/21/the-morning-download-ai-researchers-look-at-the-negative-side/,3.08
5038,"The Morning Download: Broadcom Raises Bid for Qualcomm, With Chip Market at Stake","By Steve Rosenbush Steve Rosenbush The Wall Street Journal BiographySteve Rosenbush @Steve_Rosenbush ciojournal steven.rosenbush@wsj.com Feb 5, 2018 8:13 am ET  FILE PHOTO: A sign to the campus offices of chip maker Broadcom Ltd, who announced on Monday an unsolicited bid to buy peer Qualcomm Inc for $103 billion, is shown in Irvine, California, U.S., November 6, 2017. REUTERS/Mike Blake/File Photo Photo: Reuters Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning. Subscribe to WSJ Pro Cybersecurity for in-depth coverage on cybersecurity trends, breaches and best practices.  Good morning, CIOs. Broadcom Ltd. has raised its takeover offer for Qualcomm Inc. in a deal worth more than $121 billion, turning up the pressure in what would be the largest-ever technology acquisition, the Journal's Ted Greenwald and Imani Moise report. ""Broadcom said Monday it would pay $82 a share in a mix of cash and stock, up from its initial offer of $70 a share in November,"" the Journal reports. The combined companies would be the No. 3 player in the chip market, after Intel Corp. and Samsung Electronics Co.  But why? ""The new bid ratchets up the stakes in a hostile standoff that could affect wide swaths of the markets for chips used in data centers and smartphones. Broadcom is a market leader in a variety of chips for wired and wireless devices, including Wi-Fi and Bluetooth chips for smartphones. Qualcomm is a leader in chips that manage cellular communications in smartphones,"" the Journal reports.  The battle. Qualcomm, in a letter to shareholders, emphasized the difficulty of getting the proposed merger past international regulators regardless of Broadcom's ultimate offer, the Journal says. Qualcomm, which is trying to purchase NXP Semiconductors NV, has ""in recent years has been under attack from customers and antitrust regulators who allege the company's business practices aren't fair."" Read the full story here.  Executing strategy in a complex organization. Actions and decisions that worked well in the past, when a company's strategy was relatively stable over time, may no longer apply in times of rapid change like today's digital and data-driven transformations. CIO Journal Columnist Irving Wladaswky-Berger looks at today's widespread failure to perform.  TECHNOLOGY NEWS  A Chrysler Pacifica minivan equipped with Waymo's self-driving car technology was tested as Waymo employees served as biker and pedestrian at a Waymo facility in Atwater, Calif., in October. JULIA WANG/ASSOCIATED PRESS  Uber and Waymo gear up for driverless-car trial. A legal battle starting Monday could set a new course for self-driving vehicles, as Uber Technologies Inc. defends itself against accusations that it stole technology secrets of Alphabet Inc.'s Google offshoot, Waymo. The Journal's Greg Bensiner reports on the blockbuster trial.  The allegations. Waymo alleges Uber ransacked its driverless-car design secrets after paying about $680 million in 2016 to buy autonomous-truck company Otto, founded by a former star engineer at Google. Waymo's allegations center on Uber's development of a technology used to guide self-driving vehicles known as lidar, or light detection and ranging systems.  Burden of proof. U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California Judge William Alsup has challenged Waymo attorneys to prove not just that Uber arranged for the trade secrets to be stolen, but also that it used them inappropriately in its development of lidar. That may mean convincing the jury of technical subtleties in software code and sensors.  ICYMI: Eagles went 'Moneyball.' In the lead up to Sunday's stunning Eagles win over the Patriots, the New York Times noted Philadelphia's penchant for analytics: ""Philadelphia's defensive coordinator, Jim Schwartz, who earned an economics degree at Georgetown, has long been one of the league's leading devotees of statistical analysis,"" the NYT writes, ""and some of his players said this week that they preferred studying probabilities and tendencies even before studying situations or receivers' routes."" Says defensive back Jaylen Watkins: ""We're into the analytics as much as he is.""  Early Google, Facebook alumni form group to fight social media. Some have spoken openly about the tricks social media employs to get users coming back for more. Now a group of Silicon Valley insiders, together with nonprofit media watchdog group Common Sense Media, have formed a group aimed at educating children and adults about the technology's dangers. ""The largest supercomputers in the world are inside of two companies -- Google and Facebook -- and where are we pointing them?"" Tristan Harris, a former in-house ethicist at Google, tells the New York Times. ""We're pointing them at people's brains, at children.""  The largest study ever on cellphones and cancer won't settle the debate. The final results of the study, requested by the Food and Drug Administration in 1999 and released Friday, are likely to fuel rather than dispel the debate, says the Journal's Ryan Knutson. Scientists at the National Toxicology Program, which carried out the study, said the research showed that cellphone energy did have biological effects but that it wasn't clear what it meant for humans.  Digital helps drive bank closings. U.S. banks are closing branches at the fastest pace in decades, as they leave less profitable regions and fewer customers use tellers for routine transactions, the Journal reports. The number of branches in the U.S. shrank by more than 1,700 in the 12 months ended in June 2017, the biggest decline on record, according to a Wall Street Journal analysis of federal data.  Sprint to launch 5G network in 2019. Sprint Corp.'s planned addition of a commercial fifth-generation network would put it roughly in line with its rivals' rollout plans as it charts a new course following the collapse of merger talks late last year with T-Mobile US, the WSJ's Ryan Knutson reports. While 4G is fast enough for heavy smartphone usage, the next generation has implications for other machines and devices, such as self-driving cars.  Russian extradited to U.S. to face cybercrime charges pleads not guilty. Pyotr Levashov, described by the U.S. Justice Department as one of the world's most sophisticated hackers, is being held pending trial in Connecticut federal court, the Journal's Maria Armental and David Gauthier-Villars report. U.S. authorities accuse Mr. Levashov of infecting a network of computers with a potent malware known as Kelihos and remotely controlling the network, or botnet, for criminal purposes, including ransomware attacks.  Apple, Cisco offer cyber insurance package. Cisco Systems Inc. and Apple Inc. are working with insurer Allianz SE and broker Aon Plc. to offer cyber insurance discounts to businesses that use their products, Reuters reports.  Apple set to overtake Spotify. Apple Music is on the verge of overtaking Spotify AB in U.S. paid subscribers. The WSJ's Anne Steele calls it a sign that the music-streaming world's dominant force is facing growing competition ahead of its hotly anticipated public stock offering.  New heavy rocket represents a major bet for SpaceX. The 230-foot Falcon Heavy, slated for its maiden flight on Tuesday, is more powerful than any rocket in use, the WSJ's Andy Pasztor reports. But as both national security and corporate satellites continue to get smaller and lighter, the Falcon Heavy faces uncertain commercial prospects and lacks a clear role in efforts to send U.S. astronauts back to the moon or deeper into the solar system.  WHAT YOUR CEO IS READING  Jack Dorsey, CEO of Twitter and Square DAVID PAUL MORRIS/BLOOMBERG NEWS  Silicon Valley insiders embrace tech diets. Technologists, swamped by social media updates, beeping smartphones, and a deluge of text message chatter-in short, modern life-increasingly are learning to limit their digital intake. ""The efforts show how the industry is grappling with its own concerns about the attention-sapping effects of the smartphone age,"" Bloomberg's Adam Satariano and Selina Wang report. This being Silicon Valley, the efforts go to the extreme: ""Literally, the only electricity we use is one lamp,"" says Slava Rubin, co-founder of crowdfunding site Indiegogo. And this being Silicon Valley, the new mindfulness creates new business opportunities: ""Digital detox retreats where people spend several days without technology are increasingly popular among tech workers, as is meditation. Jack Dorsey, the CEO of Twitter and Square Inc., recently finished a 10-day silent meditation that strictly prohibits any communication.""  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  Japan's reputation for flawless manufacturing quality and efficiency transformed the country's postwar economy and spawned a library's worth of management manuals and business advice books. Now, the model is cracking. (WSJ)  A selloff in world stocks deepened as expectations of rising inflation and a sudden climb in government bond yields interrupted a recent rally. (WSJ)  Most of the U.S. West has slipped back into the drought conditions that have plagued it on and off for the past two decades -- alarming water managers across several states. (WSJ)  Philadelphia Eagles won Super Bowl LII, holding off the Patriots 41-33 in a game that shattered Super Bowl records. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-02-05,eng,wsj.com,0.1058823529411765,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/02/05/the-morning-download-broadcom-raises-bid-for-qualcomm-with-chip-market-at-stake/,3.08
5039,The Morning Download: Millennial IT Buyers Do Things Differently,"By Steve Rosenbush Steve Rosenbush The Wall Street Journal BiographySteve Rosenbush @Steve_Rosenbush ciojournal steven.rosenbush@wsj.com Jan 17, 2018 7:48 am ET  Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning.  Good morning. It's been more than a year since millennial-aged workers became the largest cohort in the labor force, and their influence has manifested itself in many interesting ways. The purchase of IT systems is changing, thanks to their growing presence.  Thomas Saueressig, SAP SE's 32-year-old chief information officer, looks for commitment when evaluating vendor pitches. Technology firms that get his attention are those ""with which I feel confident I can build a long-term strategic partnership,"" Mr. Saueressig, one of the corporate world's youngest CIOs, told CIO Journal's Angus Loten in an email.  That kind of brand loyalty is far less important to older IT buyers, according to new research by Spiceworks Inc., a networking platform for IT professionals, which identified key generational differences in how enterprise IT buyers find and engage with technology vendors. At the end of 2016, the number of millennials in the U.S. workforce overtook Generation X workers, by a margin of 58.1 million to 52.9 million, according to Pew Research Center. At the executive level, CIOs make up the youngest group in the C-suite, with an average age of 51, a Korn Ferry study reported last year. Don't miss the full story, which you can read here.  Voice assistants to draw customers away from websites, physical stores. As consumers increasingly tap voice assistants instead of websites or mobile apps, companies should start thinking about how they'll add value to the customer experience and gain a competitive edge, Mark Taylor, Capgemini's executive vice president and global lead of digital customer experience, tells CIO Journal's Sara Castellanos. ""A brand today is an image, a set of colors, something you see on TV, on a website or in a store. With a voice channel, you see nothing, so a brand needs to have an audible image,"" Mr. Taylor said.  Subscribe to WSJ Pro Cybersecurity. Read more of our in-depth coverage on cybersecurity trends, breaches and best practices to help mitigate risks and defend against cyber threats.  SECURITY AND PRIVACY  A theater in Atlanta announcing the controversial Sony Pictures Entertainment movie ""The Interview"" in 2014. Shortly after, the U.S. instituted new sanctions against North Korea in response to the hacking of Sony, which the Federal Bureau of Investigation later attributed to Pyongyang. ERIK S. LESSER/EUROPEAN PRESSPHOTO AGENCY  New cyberattack on cryptocurrency investors came from North Korea. A new hacking offensive against cryptocurrency investors uses malware similar to that deployed in North Korea's attack on Sony Pictures Entertainment and its WannaCry ransomware assault. U.S. cybersecurity firm Recorded Future in a report Tuesday identified the Lazarus group -- a hacking operation with links to the North Korean regime -- as behind the malware campaign, which began targeting users of a South Korean exchange in the late fall and may still be active. It isn't known how successful the hackers were, or how much was stolen, the WSJ's Jonathan Cheng reports.  EUROPE  Residential and commercial properties stand on the syline at dusk in a view from the Bunker del Carmel, in Barcelona, Nov. 5, 2017. Angel Garcia/Bloomberg  Barcelona shifts to open source. As citizens in Catalonia look for more sovereignty from Spain, the city of Barcelona is looking to shake itself loose from a far more powerful empire: Microsoft Corp. ZDNet reports that the city plans to swap out some key Windows applications, including Exchange Server, for Linux-based apps. The city has said that it plans to spend 70% of its software budget on open-source software by 2019.  What you missed in Munich. Remember the Bavarian city's 2003 decision to drop Microsoft in favor of open source made news? TechRepublic reported in November that the city is returning to Windows with the expected roll out of 29,000 Windows 10 PCs.  NET NEUTRALITY  New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman and attorneys general from other states filed an appeal Tuesday to the Federal Communications Commission's rollback of net-neutrality rules. JOE PENNEY/REUTERS  Push for net neutrality engages all fronts. A total of 50 U.S. Senators--all 49 Democrats and one Republican--have signed on to roll back the Federal Communications Commission's own roll back of 2015's net neutrality rules. According to the Congressional Review Act, a simple majority of Congress is all that's required to overturn a recent federal agency order. But even if pro-neutrality forces succeed in the Senate, the House is a different story, the New York Times reports, with 218 signatures needed to send it to the floor. Currently 80 Democrats are in favor.  Joining the fight. New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman and more than 20 other officials filed their combined petition in the U.S. Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit, the WSJ's John D. McKinnon reports. A number of internet activist groups filed similar challenges on Tuesday, including Mozilla Corp. and the Open Technology Institute.  MORE TECHNOLOGY NEWS  DAMIEN MEYER/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE/GETTY IMAGES  Are Facebook and Google today's version of Standard Oil and AT&T? Not quite, given that today's massive software companies give away many of their products for free. Yet there are also important parallels, says the WSJ's Greg Ip, who looks at the case for breaking up big tech. ""The monopolies of old and of today were built on proprietary technology and physical networks that drove down costs while locking in customers, erecting formidable barriers to entry,"" he writes. ""Just as Standard Oil and AT&T were once critical to the nation's economic infrastructure, today's tech giants are gatekeepers to the internet economy. If they're imposing a cost, it may not be what customers pay but the products they never see.""  It takes an Amazon to fix Atlanta traffic. The e-commerce giant's contest to find a place for its second headquarters is spurring civic leaders around the country to confront municipal problems that have confounded lawmakers and local leaders for decades, the WSJ reports. Take Atlanta. Lawmakers there are considering providing state funding for public transit improvements to ease its notorious traffic problems.  Lawmakers pressure companies on telecommunication partnerships. Reuters reports that U.S. lawmakers are warning companies that they may see less business from the U.S. government if they decide to work with Huawei Technologies Co. Ltd. or China Mobile Ltd. China-based telecommunication firm Huawei in particular has been targeted as a cyber risk, given its ties to the Chinese government. AT&T Inc. last week dropped a planned commitment to sell Huawei handsets to customers.  Bitcoin down. The price of bitcoin plunged by as much as 25% on Tuesday amid concerns about tighter regulation, with the volatile virtual currency dropping below $11,000 for the first time since early December.  South Korea petitions for 'happiness.' South Korea is behind an estimated 20% of global bitcoin trades, says Quartz. And as the government explores regulation to control or at least tamp down the cryptocurrency craze, a petition questioning their actions has attracted hundreds of thousands of signatures. Reads the petition: ""Korean people can dream a happy dream that we've never been able to in South Korea, thanks to cryptocurrencies.""  Of course. Bunny Ranch, a ""world-famous"" legal brothel in Nevada, is looking into accepting bitcoin as payment, Quartz reports.  Of course, part two. Mark Cuban, owner of the Dallas Mavericks, says that the team soon will accept bitcoin and other crypto payments for game tickets, Bloomberg reports.  YouTube subjecting all 'preferred' content to human review. To ease advertisers' worries that their brands are showing up alongside offensive or controversial videos, YouTube is ordering workers to review thousands of hours of its most popular content, the Journal's Jack Nicas reports. YouTube said Tuesday that human reviewers would watch every second of video in its curated lineup of top content, dubbed Google Preferred, which brands pay a premium to advertise on. The moves come after a series of scandals involving ads from prominent brands displayed alongside racist or other objectionable content.  Google, Coursera join to train tomorrow's IT workers. A Google executive tells Fortune that the online coursework, developed by Google's employees, is aimed at underserved communities, including veterans and refugees.  Orange you ready to drive your Tesla? Over the weekend a video appeared on YouTube showing an navel orange driving a Tesla Model S. Motherboard reports that the videographer, acting on a hunch that a certain amount of pressure was required to keep Tesla's Autosteer driving function engaged, wedged a Citrus Sinensis into the steering wheel. The video has since been removed.  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  Some of the biggest U.S. companies are promising significant annual savings, bigger pension contributions, higher dividend payments and more extensive stock buybacks as executives start to discuss the impact of the federal tax overhaul. (WSJ)  General Electric is considering breaking itself apart, a dramatic move that would mark the end of one of the oldest and largest U.S. conglomerates. (WSJ)  Senators worked Tuesday to salvage a bipartisan plan to protect 'Dreamers' as the divide grew over an immigration deal seen as key to avoiding a government shutdown this weekend. (WSJ)  Dallas Fed President Robert Kaplan said he expects the U.S. central bank will need to raise interest rates three times this year and perhaps even more to prevent a robust economy from overheating. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Tom Loftus and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-01-17,eng,wsj.com,0.2941176470588236,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/01/17/the-morning-download-millennial-it-buyers-do-things-differently/,3.08
5040,"New Cyberattack on Cryptocurrency Investors Came From North Korea, Report Says","SEOUL -- A new hacking offensive against cryptocurrency investors uses malware similar to that deployed in North Korea's attack on Sony Pictures Entertainment and its WannaCry ransomware assault, cybersecurity researchers said, providing further evidence of Pyongyang's involvement in crypto heists.  U.S. cybersecurity firm Recorded Future in a report on Tuesday identified the Lazarus group -- a hacking operation with links to the North Korean regime -- as behind the malware campaign, which began targeting users of a South Korean exchange...",2018-01-17,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-cyberattack-on-cryptocurrency-investors-came-from-north-korea-report-says-1516107603,3.08
5041,"WSJ City: Crypto Heists Blamed on North Korea, Are US Stocks Rallying Too Hard?","By Joe Wallace Jan 17, 2018 3:20 am ET  Good morning from the WSJ City desks in London. WSJ City is the app that delivers concise, smart news on business and finance for mobile. Download for iPhone or Android. Here's essential reading on today's developments.  MUST READS FROM WSJ CITY  A new hacking offensive against cryptocurrency investors uses malware similar to that deployed in North Korea's attack on Sony Pictures Entertainment and its WannaCry ransomware assault, cybersecurity researchers said. Their report provides further evidence of North Korea's involvement in crypto heists, which Pyongyang seems to be developing as a funding stream.  Stocks can't rise forever, but the bad news for bears is that few clear ""sell"" signals have materialised, technicians say. A volcanic final phase for stocks, known as a melt-up, would be consistent with previous bull markets.  Steve Bannon, the once-close confidant who is now estranged from President Donald Trump, refused to answer some questions despite being subpoenaed by the House Intelligence Committee while he was giving testimony, mostly regarding its Russia probe. The New York Times reported that Bannon has also been issued a separate subpoena by special counsel Robert Mueller as part of his criminal investigation.  The first, long-awaited blockchain ETFs launch this week. But, in a last-minute intervention, the Securities and Exchange Commission has insisted the funds lose the words blockchain from their names, the two ETF firms said.  Big stock-market gains and ultra-low volatility are forcing hedge funds to look outside of equity and bond markets for returns, and they are taking bigger and riskier bets on foreign-exchange markets, FN reports. Here are five currency bets hedge funds will be making this year.  Since Fleet Street's newspaper heyday, banks and professional services firms have based themselves in the area. But now several big names are on the move, FN reports. That could mean a great opportunity for developers and businesses alike.  Big tech has a new opportunity to disrupt the big banks. A tussle to create a killer banking app is shaping up, writes Stephen Wilmot for Heard on the Street.  IN THE PAPERS  The UK government has put FTSE 250 contractor Interserve on financial health watch following Carillion's collapse. FT (£)  Google is expanding its sprawling network of undersea cables to plug into new regions around the world, in a bid to speed up its cloud-computing business and catch up to rivals Microsoft and Amazon.com. WSJ  MP Jacob Rees-Mogg has been elected unopposed to lead the influential pro-Brexit backbench organisation the European Research Group. Politico  The UK will miss its legally binding carbon targets without urgent government action, official advisers have warned. Guardian  A former CIA officer suspected by investigators of helping China dismantle United States spying operations and identify informants has been arrested, the Justice Department said on Tuesday. NYT  MARKETS TODAY  European stocks opened lower on Wednesday, after Asian shares weakened and Wall Street faded following an early surge. The Stoxx Europe 600 was down 0.3% in the first minutes of trading, while London's FTSE 100 fell 0.2%.  Shares in GKN were stable, up 0.5%, after turnaround specialist Melrose Industries went hostile with a £7.4 billion cash and share offer for one of Britain's oldest companies.  Corporate headlines drove many of the declines in London: publisher Informa fell nearly 7% after it outlined a proposed merger with business-events firm UBM, Burberry shares were down more than 4% after it said that retail sales fell 2% in the third quarter, and Pearson fell more than 5% after a trading update.  The Dow Jones Industrial Average surged above 26000 for the first time on Tuesday, but gave up those gains as shares of energy and chemical companies declined. Asian stocks closed mostly lower following Wall Street's late sell-off.  The price of cryptocurrency bitcoin rose nearly 4% following a rout which saw it lose more than a quarter of its value on Tuesday.  COMING UP  Eurozone inflation; World Economic Forum's Global Risk report; Catalan parliament meets for first time since election; results from Bank of America and Goldman Sachs.",2018-01-17,eng,wsj.com,0.0039215686274509,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/2018/01/17/wsj-city-crypto-heists-blamed-on-north-korea-bannon-reticent-on-russia/,3.08
5042,"The Morning Download: Spectre, Meltdown Response Demands 'No System Left Behind'","By Tom Loftus Tom Loftus The Wall Street Journal BiographyTom Loftus thomas.loftus@wsj.com Jan 4, 2018 8:23 am ET  In this July 20, 2011, photo, Intel Corp. offices are seen in Santa Clara, Calif. Intel says it is working to patch a security vulnerability in its products but says the average computer user will not experience significant slowdowns as the problem is fixed. Photo: Associated Press Sign up here for The Morning Download, and get the most important news in business technology emailed to you each weekday morning.  Good morning. As the Northeast wakes up today to a ""weather bomb,"" the entire world is having its own computing ""bombogenesis"" moment upon news of the discovery of two major security flaws. Researchers have identified two major computer chip security flaws, Spectre and Meltdown, that leave modern computing devices, from desktop computers and smartphones to internet servers, open to attack. (Much more on that below.) Vulnerable are devices containing chips from Intel Corp., as well as from Advanced Micro Devices Inc. and ARM Holdings. If you have to ask whether your systems are affected, the answer is ""most certainly, yes,"" according to Graz University of Technology in Austria, which provides a handy guide on what companies should do next. Shuman Ghosemajumder, CTO of Shape Security and former click-fraud specialist at Google, talked to CIO Journal, offering more guidance:  Fundamentally, a big problem. ""These defects create a fundamental vulnerability in much of the computing infrastructure used around the world, potentially allowing attackers to steal data and execute arbitrary code on vulnerable systems,"" Mr. Ghosemajumder says. ""The idea of a CPU flaw introducing a fundamental security vulnerability ... which can allow attackers to bypass many other security controls,"" he says, ""is a long-time fear.""  Patch everything. ""Probably the biggest concern here is the scope of the vulnerability, and how it affects clients and servers alike, across operating systems,"" Mr. Ghosemajumder says. ""Not only are end-user systems and corporate devices vulnerable, but Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud Platform, and other cloud computing environments using Intel chips may be vulnerable, as are other Linux and Windows systems which run the world's web applications.""  No system left behind. ""Every time a fundamental vulnerability is discovered that affects many systems, even with patching, some users get left behind in a state of permanent vulnerability,"" he warns. ""This particular flaw will make older computers and devices even more vulnerable, similar to what happened with other security vulnerabilities which require OS patching, like the EternalBlue vulnerability exploited by WannaCry ransomware and NotPetya malware.""  Schneider Electric adopts edge computing for new services. Industrial giant Schneider Electric SE plans a range of services, including predictive maintenance on oil pumps, that it will sell to customers, reports CIO Journal's Sara Castellanos. The company started experimenting with Microsoft Corp.'s Azure IoT Edge product in 2017 to connect Schneider's industrial devices in remote locations to gateways that relay data to corporate systems. By 2021, 40% of enterprises will have an edge computing strategy in place, up from about 1% in 2017, according to Gartner Inc.  AI is the new ERP, sort of. It will take years to adopt and scale artificial intelligence in enterprise computing, similar to the trajectory of enterprise resource planning software in the 1990s, says MIT professor Erik Brynjolfsson, who directs the MIT Initiative on the Digital Economy. ""It really has to do with the organizational side, the culture, and the co-invention of business processes that takes a lot longer. CIOs know this,"" he said in an interview with CIO Journal's Steven Norton. It's rare that whole jobs will be usurped by AI. Rather, humans and machines will work together after companies reallocate pieces of jobs, Dr. Brynjolfsson said.  SECURITY AND PRIVACY  An exhibitor shows an Android One X3 smartphone in Tokyo on Nov. 30, 2017. Google said users of its Android operating system who have installed the latest security fixes didn't need to do anything else to address newly discovered flaws. RODRIGO REYES MARIN/ZUMA PRESS  Researchers identify two major security flaws. Security researchers, including Google's Project Zero, have identified two major computer chip security flaws, Spectre and Meltdown, that leave modern computing devices, from desktop computers and smartphones to internet servers, open to attack. Vulnerable are devices containing chips from Intel Corp., as well as from Advanced Micro Devices Inc. and ARM Holdings. Device vendors and cloud services are currently issuing patches to resolve some of the issues, although the vulnerabilities take advantage of how computer chips execute instructions. According to Graz University of Technology in Austria, whose researchers played a role identifying the flaws, the bugs ""allow programs to steal data which is currently processed on the computer. While programs are typically not permitted to read data from other programs, a malicious program can exploit Meltdown and Spectre to get hold of secrets stored in the memory of other running programs."" Depending on a cloud provider's infrastructure, the researchers continue, ""it might be possible to steal data from other customers.""  How big are we talking, here? ""At their heart, both attacks takes advantage of the fact that processors execute instructions speculatively,""says Ars Technica. Says Graz University of Technology: ""Meltdown breaks the mechanism that keeps applications from accessing arbitrary system memory. Consequently, applications can access system memory. Spectre tricks other applications into accessing arbitrary locations in their memory."" Shorter answer: If you are asking if you are affected, the answer is probably yes.  Meltdown. The flaw, which affects all Intel chips built since 1996, according to ZDNet, is easier to mitigate, but software patches could slow computer performance ""by as much as 30 percent,""according to the New York Times, which adds that ""Meltdown is a particular problem for the cloud computing services run by the likes of Amazon, Google and Microsoft.  Spectre. The good news. It's harder to exploit. The bad. A fix may be more difficult as the chip design flaw has been used for decades. ""It will haunt us for quite some time,""says Graz University.  Google, Microsoft addressed flaws Wednesday night. That's according to the New York Times, which reported that Meltdown is a ""particular problem"" for cloud computing services. As for Spectre, Google said that bug ""is harder to exploit than Meltdown, but it's also harder to mitigate.""  AWS encourages users to update software. ""All but a small single-digit percentage of instances across the Amazon EC2 fleet are already protected,"" the company said in a bulletin Wednesday, adding that the rest of AWS' users would be protected within hours.  Apple addressed bug in a December software update. Another tweak meant to protect users from cybercriminals aiming to exploit Spectre and Meltdown is coming in the form of macOS High Sierra 10.13.13, company sources told AppleInsider.  NSA bleeding talent. The National Security Agency has lost several hundred hackers, scientists and other cyber experts since 2015, an attrition rate that could impede the spy agency's mission of collecting and analyzing data from foreign sources, the Washington Post reports. Former employees blame a number of factors, from pay issues to a years-long restructuring program combining its secret spying mission with its more public defense work. Not helping worker morale: The recent disclosure of breaches targeting the NSA's top secret spying tools.  LEADERSHIP  Papa John's founder John Schnatter was among the CEOs who announced they were stepping down from the role. DANNY MOLOSHOK/REUTERS  Changing of the guard brings new tech priorities. Last year saw 919 chief executives resigning, retiring or getting fired from public North American companies, the highest number in at least a decade, the Journal's Joanna S. Lublin and Vanessa Fuhrmans report. New faces in the corner office could signify new demands (and opportunities) for IT leadership. According to interviews with nearly a dozen CEOs, cybersecurity threats, disruptive technologies and stiffer competition for talent rank among their most pressing issues in the new year.  Minnesota state CIO stepping down. Tom Baden, who leads Minnesota's information technology services, is retiring as state chief information officer effective Feb. 2, the Minneapolis Star Tribune reports. A 36-year state government veteran, Mr. Baden said he is leaving to recover from ""unforeseen complications stemming from some health issues,"" the newspaper said. He has served as IT services commissioner and CIO since 2015, overseeing a staff of roughly 2,200, and had previously led the team that handled the rollout of the state's health insurance exchange.  MORE TECHNOLOGY NEWS  A payment made using WeChat in Bangkok. WeChat and rival Alipay have about 90% of the mobile-payment market in China and want to expand in other countries. XINHUA/ZUMA PRESS  In China, panhandlers 'recommend using WeChat Pay.' Mobile payments are fast becoming the way people in China pay for just about everything, the Journal's Alyssa Abkowitz reports. While the U.S. saw about $112 billion in mobile payments in 2016, according to Forrester, such payments in China totaled $9 trillion, according to one Chinese research group. Behind the trend are internet titans Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and Tencent Holdings Ltd., which are elbowing aside banks to take a growing role in daily commerce.  Cryptocurrencies for the nation-state. Russian and Venezuelan officials hope cryptocurrencies can help them sidestep American sanctions, the New York Times reports. Both governments are working to create state-sponsored virtual currencies they hope will give their countries a new kind of financial infrastructure outside the control of a central authority.  Bitcoin draws varying response from regulators globally. Regulators, in part worried about a bitcoin frenzy, have moved to curb action in the market. In the U.S., trading of bitcoin futures was approved last year but officials from Federal Reserve Chairwoman Janet Yellen down have warned of potential harm to retail investors, reports the Journal. In China, authorities shut down the country's biggest cryptocurrency exchanges last autumn. South Korean government officials are considering doing the same.  Chinese chipmakers want to dominate AI. Google and Intel Corp. face strengthening competition from computer chip makers in China that seek to build processors tailored for a growing artificial intelligence market, reports the Journal's Li Yuan. ""China has the capital, the talent, a huge consumer market and -- crucially -- rafts of data that fuel AI programs. Another huge plus: the government has set its sights on making China an AI powerhouse,"" she writes.  Verizon, Samsung will team up to bring faster 5G to California. Samsung Electronic Co. was chosen as a major supplier in the telecom giant's push to offer 5G internet over its wireless network. Verizon's 5G network will launch in the second half of this year in Sacramento, Calif., before adding the service in other U.S. markets, the WSJ's Timothy W. Martin and Ryan Knutson explain. It will use cellular antennas to beam high-speed internet into consumers' homes. AT&T Corp. plans to launch 5G service in the U.S. this year, Reuters reports.  Baidu, BlackBerry join in driverless car effort. China's Google, in the middle of its own autonomous car effort, plans to bundle BlackBerry's ONX vehicle OS into its own car platform, called Apollo, Bloomberg reports.  Google explores sale of Zagat. Alphabet Inc.'s Google unit is eyeing a potential sale of the U.S. restaurant review guide it bought for $151 million in 2011, sources familiar with the matter tell Reuters. Google has held informal talks with multiple firms about offloading the company, which became less of a priority as Google worked to bring more users to its Google+ social network.  Spotify files to go public, cutting out underwriters. Music streaming service Spotify AB confidentially filed paperwork with the SEC to list its shares on the New York Stock Exchange, a person familiar with the listing tells the Journal. It's the latest step in the company's plans to go public using an unusual method known as a direct listing, in which a company transfers its shares to an exchange without raising money as is done in a typical IPO.  EVERYTHING ELSE YOU NEED TO KNOW  An underground gun-making industry enables criminals to elude background checks. (WSJ)  Bitcoin is a hit in countries where locals face currency troubles, including Sudan and South Africa. (WSJ)  Annual U.S. car sales drop for the first time since the financial crisis. (WSJ)  Subaru Corp.'s plan to woo America: An SUV with 19 cup holders. (WSJ)  The Morning Download is edited by Kim S. Nash and cues up the most important news in business technology every weekday morning. You can get The Morning Download emailed to you each weekday morning by clicking http://wsj.com/TheMorningDownload.",2018-01-04,eng,wsj.com,-0.0352941176470588,false,news,https://blogs.wsj.com/cio/2018/01/04/the-morning-download-spectre-meltdown-flaws-demand-no-system-behind-response/,3.08
5043,North Korea Is Suspected in Bitcoin Heist,"Pyongyang's hackers turn to cryptocurrency and banks as Kim regime hunts for funds By Timothy W. Martin, Timothy W. Martin The Wall Street Journal BiographyTimothy W. Martin @timothywmartin Timothy.Martin@wsj.com Eun-Young Jeong and Eun-Young Jeong The Wall Street Journal BiographyEun-Young Jeong eun-young.jeong@wsj.com Steven Russolillo Steven Russolillo The Wall Street Journal BiographySteven Russolillo @srussolillo steve.russolillo Steven.Russolillo@wsj.com December 21, 2017  SEOUL -- Investigators in South Korea are looking into North Korea's possible involvement in a heist from a bitcoin exchange that collapsed here on Tuesday, according to people familiar with the situation, as the sanctions-choked regime develops new ways to raise money.  The investigation into the hack of Seoul-based exchange Youbit, led by South Korean law enforcement and a state cybersecurity agency, is still in its infancy and a review of the malware code could take weeks, the people said.  But the people said there were telltale signs and historical evidence that North Korea was behind the Youbit attack. North Korean hackers in April targeted the same cryptocurrency exchange, operating under a different name, several of the people said. Yapian, the company that operates Youbit, suspended trading and filed for bankruptcy after Tuesday's hack.  The bitcoin heist follows similar suspected Pyongyang-directed offensives against other South Korean cryptocurrency exchanges -- and an increasing number of attempts to steal from individual investors.  On Tuesday, the White House said North Korea directed this year's WannaCry ransomware attack, which locked digital files and demanded bitcoin payment for their release.  South Korean police and the Korea Internet & Security Agency said they had begun an investigation into the Youbit hack but were still determining the scope of the situation.  A North Korean cyber army of 7,000 hackers around the world has shifted tactics over the past two years to become more motivated by financial gain, pilfering from banks and, more recently, focusing on cryptocurrencies, according to cybersecurity researchers. North Korea has denied involvement in the hacking incidents.  North Korean leader Kim Jong Un has a critical need for funds as his regime advances its nuclear-weapons program in the face of tightened economic sanctions.  ""North Korea is an ideal country to use hacking and financial tools like bitcoin,"" said Troy Stangarone, a senior director at the Korea Economic Institute in Washington. ""They're experimenting with ways to earn back lost money from sanctions.""  The bitcoin craze has created a unique opportunity, as a rush of new investors bet on a market they had barely heard of until recently, said Ryan Kalember, a senior vice president at Proofpoint Inc., a cybersecurity firm that published a recent report detailing Pyongyang's bitcoin campaigns.  ""Much of the cryptocurrency system is highly vulnerable,"" Mr. Kalember said. ""Because this world is moving so fast and now it's so lucrative, it's really exactly what a cybercriminal"" is looking for.  Related reading  What North Korea Might Do With Bitcoin Cryptocurrency Exchange Collapses, Files for Bankruptcy After Second Hack Hackers Just Stole $66,000 in Bitcoin. Now What? Good News! You Are a Bitcoin Millionaire. Bad News! You Forgot Your Password Hackers' Latest Weapon: Cyber Extortion Digital Clue Links North Korea to Theft at New York Fed, Security Firm Says  The bitcoin itself is supposed to be secure and safeguarded by a unique encryption code. But cyberthieves have breached cryptocurrency exchanges or so-called digital wallets, stealing encrypted passwords as well as bitcoins.  For North Korea, stealing bitcoin could be an attractive endeavor because of the cryptocurrency's ability to rise sharply in value over a relatively short period. The price of one bitcoin started this year at just under $1,000 and has experienced a frenetic rally, with choppy price swings, to as high as nearly $20,000 this past weekend.  The moves in bitcoin and other digital currencies starkly contrast most traditional financial markets in 2017, like stocks and bonds, where volatility has been historically low.  For average consumers, online marketplaces can convert bitcoin into regular cash that can be sent to bank accounts. But North Korea is allegedly swiping vast sums of bitcoin -- significantly more than individuals typically own -- and must also cover its tracks.  To do that, North Korea, in theory, could divvy up the bitcoin bounty into different accounts, then move the smaller sums in and out of different cryptocurrency exchanges. Each transfer would further erode the links to the original owners. Eventually, North Korea could create enough anonymity to cash out the bitcoin like anyone else.  South Korea is among the most active bitcoin markets, ranking No. 3 after the U.S. and Japan in terms of national currency trade volume, according to data firm Coinhills, which tracks digital currencies. South Korea has no legal protections for consumers who become victims of exchange hackings, unlike with its banks and securities firms, said Ahn Chan-sik, an attorney at Seoul law firm HMP Law.  But lax security protocols haven't repelled investors, a concern even for virtual currencies' biggest advocates. ""Investors need to be more alert,"" said Kim Hwa-joon, the co-head of a cryptocurrency exchange industry group. ""Consumers need to be more sensitive about where they put their money.""  Exchanges are aware that security breaches present serious risks. One of South Korea's top exchanges by trading volume, Coinone, is headed by a former hacker. Since 2017, the company has worked with hackers who conduct mock attacks to try to breach its system.  Pyongyang's cyberwarfare capabilities first drew global attention for the hack of Sony Pictures Entertainment in 2014, when the regime's cyber goals were more focused on obtaining military information, destabilizing networks or intimidating opponents.  North Korea has turned in recent years to increasingly sophisticated financial warfare.  North Korea was blamed for last year's cybertheft of $81 million from Bangladesh's central bank, followed by a $60 million theft this year from a Taiwanese bank. Cybersecurity researchers say North Korea was involved in other attacks in Mexico, Poland, India and the U.K.  The hackers have executed scams such as planting viruses into South Korean ATMs or point-of-sale devices to steal personal information, according to researchers. South Korean government groups and agencies withstand 1.5 million daily North Korean hacking attempts, law-enforcement and intelligence officials said.  Pyongyang's bitcoin interest has taken root more recently. North Korea targeted three cryptocurrency exchanges in South Korea between April and October, people familiar with that investigation said. That includes an April attack on a predecessor to Youbit, then known as Yapizon.  Steve Lim, chief strategy officer at the Coinone exchange, doesn't think the Youbit incident alone will dramatically curb bitcoin interest. ""People will shrug it off in a few months' time, as long as there aren't any more of these kinds of incidents,"" he said.  Yapian said the security breach caused it to lose 17% of its total assets, but didn't specify the financial value of the losses. The value of the heist is difficult to determine as exchanges aren't required to provide information about their operations beyond registering their business when they first open.  On Dec. 1, Youbit purchased a one-year cyberinsurance policy from DB Insurance Co. , a South Korean firm, with damage coverage up to about $2.8 million.  A spokesman at DB Insurance said Youbit hasn't filed a claim. Youbit has up to three years to do so.  Write to Timothy W. Martin at timothy.martin@wsj.com, Eun-Young Jeong at Eun-Young.Jeong@wsj.com and Steven Russolillo at steven.russolillo@wsj.com  Corrections & Amplifications  Youbit purchased a one-year cyberinsurance policy from DB Insurance with damage coverage up to about $2.8 million. An earlier version of this article incorrectly stated that the damage coverage was $2.8 billion. (March 29)",2017-12-21,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/north-korea-is-suspected-in-bitcoin-heist-1522334116,4.62
5044,North Korea Is Suspected in Bitcoin Heist ,"By Timothy W. Martin, Eun-Young Jeong and Steven Russolillo December 20, 2017  SEOUL -- Investigators in South Korea are looking into North Korea's possible involvement in a heist from a bitcoin exchange that collapsed here on Tuesday, according to people familiar with the situation, as the sanctions-choked regime develops new ways to raise money.  The investigation into the hack of Seoul-based exchange Youbit, led by South Korean law enforcement and a state cybersecurity agency, is still in its infancy and a review of the malware code could take weeks, the people said.  But the people said there were telltale signs and historical evidence that North Korea was behind the Youbit attack. North Korean hackers in April targeted the same cryptocurrency exchange, operating under a different name, several of the people said. Yapian, the company that operates Youbit, suspended trading and filed for bankruptcy after Tuesday's hack.  The bitcoin heist follows similar suspected Pyongyang-directed offensives against other South Korean cryptocurrency exchanges -- and an increasing number of attempts to steal from individual investors.  On Tuesday, the White House said North Korea directed this year's WannaCry ransomware attack, which locked digital files and demanded bitcoin payment for their release.  South Korean police and the Korea Internet & Security Agency said they had begun an investigation into the Youbit hack but were still determining the scope of the situation.  A North Korean cyber army of 7,000 hackers around the world has shifted tactics over the past two years to become more motivated by financial gain, pilfering from banks and, more recently, focusing on cryptocurrencies, according to cybersecurity researchers. North Korea has denied involvement in the hacking incidents.  North Korean leader Kim Jong Un has a critical need for funds as his regime advances its nuclear-weapons program in the face of tightened econom