les/SB10001424052702303647204579545801399272852?mod=WSJ_GoogleNews&mg=reno64-wsj&url=http%253A%252F%252Fonline.wsj.com%252Farticle%252FSB10001424052702303647204579545801399272852.html%253Fmod%253DWSJ_GoogleNews,3.08
5256,Symantec Develops New Attack on Cyberhacking,"Boxes of McAfee security software are displayed alongside Norton Anti-virus software by Symantec on a shelf on Aug. 19, 2010 in Colma, Calif. Getty Images Symantec Corp. SYMC -1.33% Symantec Corp. U.S.: Nasdaq $20.10 -0.27 -1.33% May 5, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 4.13M AFTER HOURS $20.15 +0.05 +0.25% May 5, 2014 7:15 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 27,407 P/E Ratio 16.08 Market Cap $14.09 Billion Dividend Yield 2.99% Rev. per Employee $316,233 05/04/14 Symantec Develops New Attack o... 04/15/14 CFO Moves: Brightstar, Popeyes... 04/08/14 Symantec Models Industry Risk ...  More quote details and news "" SYMC in Your Value Your Change Short position invented commercial antivirus software to protect computers from hackers a quarter-century ago.  Now the company says such tactics are doomed to failure.  Antivirus ""is dead,"" says Brian Dye, Symantec's senior vice president for information security.  ""We don't think of antivirus as a moneymaker in any way."" "" Antivirus products aim to prevent hackers from getting into a computer.  But hackers often get in anyway these days.  So Mr. Dye is leading a reinvention effort at Symantec that reflects a broader shift in the $70 billion a year cybersecurity industry.  Rather than fighting to keep the bad guys out, new technologies from an array of companies assume hackers get in so aim to spot them and minimize the damage.  WSJD is the Journal's home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  Alibaba's Ma Takes Center Stage Apple Win Won't Hurt Samsung Google, Microsoft, Apple to Tell You if the Government Requests Your Data Web Video Deals Target Teens Online Gamers Say No to Monthly Fees Network-equipment maker Juniper Networks Inc. JNPR +0.20% Juniper Networks Inc. U.S.: NYSE $24.80 +0.05 +0.20% May 5, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 3.81M AFTER HOURS $24.80 0.00 0.00% May 5, 2014 4:24 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 4,810 P/E Ratio 26.96 Market Cap $11.72 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $504,060 05/04/14 Symantec Develops New Attack o... 04/22/14 Juniper Profits Grow 22% Amid ... 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ...  More quote details and news "" JNPR in Your Value Your Change Short position wants customers to place fake data inside their firewalls to distract hackers.  Shape Security Inc., a Silicon Valley startup, assumes that hackers will steal passwords and credit-card numbers so seeks to make it difficult to use the pilfered information.  FireEye Inc. FEYE +0.73% FireEye Inc. U.S.: Nasdaq $40.23 +0.29 +0.73% May 5, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 3.27M AFTER HOURS $40.68 +0.45 +1.12% May 5, 2014 7:56 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 20,909 P/E Ratio N/A Market Cap $5.79 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $96,276.5 05/04/14 Symantec Develops New Attack o... 05/01/14 Microsoft Browser Fix Includes... 04/28/14 Microsoft Web Browser Has Secu...  More quote details and news "" FEYE in Your Value Your Change Short position created technology that scans networks for malicious-looking computer code that made it past the first line of defense.  FireEye recently paid $1 billion for Mandiant, a small firm led by former Air Force investigators who act like cyber-Ghostbusters after a data breach.  Symantec seeks to join the fray this week.  It is creating its own response team to help hacked businesses.  Within six months, the Mountain View, Calif., company plans to sell intelligence briefings on specific threats so clients can learn not just that they are getting hacked, but why as well.  Symantec also is developing technology to look for more-advanced malicious software inside a network that mimics offerings from its rivals.  The company needs a turnaround.  Revenue fell in each of the past two quarters, though profit rose because of cost cuts.  The company, which reports earnings Thursday, forecast revenue of $1.62 billion to $1.66 billion for the quarter through March, down at least 5% from a year earlier.  The company in March fired Chief Executive Steve Bennett, the second time in two years it had ousted a CEO.  Mr. Dye, who has spent more than a decade with Symantec, says it was galling to watch other security companies surge ahead.  ""It's one thing to sit there and get frustrated,"" he says.  ""It's another thing to act on it, go get your act together and go play the game you should have been playing in the first place."" "" Symantec pioneered computer security with its antivirus software in the late 1980s.  The technology keeps hackers out by checking against a list of malicious code spotted on computers.  Think of it as an immune system for machines.  But hackers increasingly use novel bugs.  Mr. Dye estimates antivirus now catches just 45% of cyberattacks.  That puts Symantec in a pickle.  Antivirus and other products that run on individual devices still account for more than 40% of the company's revenue.  Specialized cybersecurity services for businesses account for less than one-fifth of revenue and generate smaller profit margins.  It would be impractical, if not impossible, to sell such services to individual consumers.  Ted Schlein, who helped create Symantec's first antivirus product, describes such software as ""necessary but insufficient."" "" As a partner at venture-capital firm Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers, Mr. Schlein invests in new cybersecurity companies that compete with Symantec.  Mr. Dye says Symantec's Norton security suite has evolved beyond antivirus software and already looks for suspicious activity that may come from previously unseen viruses.  It also includes, among other things, a password manager, a spam blocker and a tool that scans a user's Facebook feed to guard against dangerous links.  The company has no plans to abandon Norton but will find revenue growth in its new product lines, he says.  ""If customers are shifting from protect to detect and respond, the growth is going to come from detect and respond,"" Mr. Dye says.  Other traditional antivirus makers such as McAfee, Intel Corp.'s INTC -0.06% Intel Corp. U.S.: Nasdaq $26.17 -0.01 -0.06% May 5, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 20.54M AFTER HOURS $26.18 +0.01 +0.04% May 5, 2014 7:06 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 196,126 P/E Ratio 13.70 Market Cap $131.47 Billion Dividend Yield 3.44% Rev. per Employee $491,561 05/05/14 AMD Broadens Efforts to Exploi... 05/05/14 Intel Names Staples Executive ... 05/05/14 As Big Data Market Booms, Hado...  More quote details and news "" INTC in Your Value Your Change Short position security unit, have moved in the same direction.  Michael Fey, McAfee's chief technology officer, says there is typically a two- to three-year lag on developing the technology Symantec seeks to create.  ""They haven't been part of the thought-leader group for some time,"" Mr. Fey says.  International Business Machine Corp. IBM -0.09% International Business Machines Corp. U.S.: NYSE $191.26 -0.18 -0.09% May 5, 2014 4:02 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 2.21M AFTER HOURS $190.90 -0.36 -0.19% May 5, 2014 6:39 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 7,223 P/E Ratio 13.01 Market Cap $197.87 Billion Dividend Yield 2.30% Rev. per Employee $229,711 05/05/14 Danish Company Probed for Alle... 05/05/14 IBM's New Cybersecurity Plan: ... 05/04/14 Symantec Develops New Attack o...  More quote details and news "" IBM in Your Value Your Change Short position on Monday plans to unveil its own security suite that looks for irregular behavior in computer networks.  If Symantec has an opening, it is that no security company has determined how consistently to defeat the most ambitious hackers from China, Iran and the former Soviet bloc.  Hackers linked to Iran last spring breached the digital perimeters of energy companies and one of the U.S.'s five biggest banks but were caught before moving further into the systems.  The incidents were notable because the two industries have among the best private-sector cyberdefenses.  Cybersecurity firms also want to help discern the most serious threats from the less serious.  Before Target Corp. was breached last year, FireEye security equipment alerted the retailer to suspicious activity.  But the company decided it didn't require follow up.  Former Target employees say the team lacked the resources to pursue all threats.  ""What do we do with all the things that we're 60% sure are a problem?"" Mr. Dye says.  Analysts say Symantec's software runs on so many machines that it may be able provide more guidance on which hackers can be ignored and which are truly a problem.  -- Spencer Ante contributed to this article.  Write to Danny Yadron at danny.yadron@wsj.com",2014-05-06,eng,wsj.com,-0.5215686274509803,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/news/article_email/SB10001424052702303417104579542140235850578-lMyQjAxMTA0MDAwNTEwNDUyWj,3.08
5257,Data of About 900 Canadians Taken in Heartbleed Breach,"By Nirmala Menon Nirmala Menon The Wall Street Journal BiographyNirmala Menon @NirmalaMenon Updated April 14, 2014 7:26 p.m. ET  OTTAWA -- Canadian authorities said Monday that hackers stole the confidential information of about 900 Canadian taxpayers by exploiting the Heartbleed bug, but they were tight-lipped about how and when the Internet security breach occurred.  Canada's national police force was investigating the breach, the government said.  The news came a day after Ottawa reopened access to government websites, and the Canada Revenue Agency, the country's tax authority, said that online tax filing could resume after being suspended for about five days while the agency worked to fix its vulnerability to the software-encryption flaw. The CRA had made no mention of the breach when it reopened access to electronic tax filing Sunday, even though it said on Monday that it knew of the breach last Friday.  Canada had called the temporary shutdown of the CRA tax-filing service and some other websites a precautionary measure.  Heartbleed, a flaw in encryption software known as Open SSL, can leak key information such as passwords and financial information that could be exploited by hackers.  Monday, the CRA said that some 900 social insurance numbers -- akin to U.S. Social Security numbers -- were taken from its online tax-filing systems during a six-hour period, an act that it described as ""malicious.""  The agency didn't specify how or when the breach occurred, and government officials declined to provide details. Last year, 20 million of the 26.1 million returns received by CRA were filed electronically.  ""We are currently going through the painstaking process of analyzing other fragments of data, some that may relate to businesses, that were also removed,"" the agency's commissioner, Andrew Treusch, said in a statement.  He said that the Royal Canadian Mounted Police was investigating and that he had informed the Privacy Commissioner of Canada of the breach on Friday. A CRA spokeswoman said Mr. Treusch wasn't giving interviews.  An RCMP spokeswoman said the matter has been assigned to its national division, which is responsible for ""sensitive, high-risk investigations into significant threats to Canada's political, economic and social integrity."" She declined to provide more details.  A spokeswoman for National Revenue Minister Kerry-Lynne Findlay, who oversees the CRA, said the ""malicious breach"" was ""contained"" to the six-hour period but declined to provide more details, citing security reasons.  The Conservative government has ""failed to provide Canadians with basic information, such as what kind of data the hackers accessed, what their plan is to protect people who filed their taxes online, and how they will ensure that this does not happen again,"" Murray Rankin, a member of the opposition New Democratic Party, said in a statement.  The office of Canada's federal privacy commissioner is closely monitoring technical developments as they emerge to better understand ""the potential privacy implications of Heartbleed,"" a spokeswoman said.  Experts said the nature of the Heartbleed bug means it is very difficult to know when an attack occurs. Justin Bull, a Toronto-based software developer who believes he was one of the first to inform the CRA that its website was vulnerable, speculated that hackers may have gained access to CRA employees' passwords to get access to taxpayers' personal information.  But cybersecurity expert Satyamoorthy Kabilan said this would have meant many more organizations would have been affected. He said the hackers may have been able to exploit a vulnerability in communications between the user and the CRA servers. Investigators likely found that such communications had been compromised and the attack was coming from a different country or a different IP address, he said.  He added that Ottawa acted ""appropriately"" on the matter.  ""One of the biggest issues we have in cybersecurity is people hiding problems in the hope they can fix it,"" said Mr. Kabilan, director of National Security and Strategic Foresight at the Conference Board of Canada, a think tank.  Meanwhile, the CRA was going old school to inform the people whose information may have been compromised. It will send them registered letters to inform them of the breach.  -- Judy McKinnon contributed to this article.  Write to Nirmala Menon at nirmala.menon@wsj.com",2014-04-14,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/data-of-about-900-canadians-taken-in-heartbleed-breach-1397480995,3.08
5258,Data of About 900 Canadians Taken in Heartbleed Breach -- Update,"OTTAWA--Canadian authorities Monday said hackers stole the confidential information of about 900 Canadian taxpayers by exploiting the Heartbleed bug, but were tight-lipped about how and when the Internet security breach occurred.  Canada's national police force was investigating the breach, the government said.  The news came a day after Ottawa reopened access to government websites and the Canada Revenue Agency, the country's tax authority, said online tax filing could resume after being suspended for about five days while it worked to fix its vulnerability to the software encryption flaw.  The CRA had made no mention of the breach when it reopened access to electronic tax filing Sunday, even though it said on Monday that it knew of the breach last Friday.  Canada had called the temporary shutdown of the CRA tax-filing service and some other websites a precautionary measure.  Heartbleed, a flaw in encryption software known as Open SSL, could leak key information such as passwords and financial information that could be exploited by hackers.  Monday, the CRA said some 900 social insurance numbers--akin to U.S. social security numbers--were taken from its online tax-filing systems during a six-hour period, an act which it described as ""malicious."" "" The agency didn't specify how or when the breach occurred, and government officials declined to provide details.  ""We are currently going through the painstaking process of analyzing other fragments of data, some that may relate to businesses, that were also removed,"" the agency's commissioner, Andrew Treusch, said in a statement.  He said the Royal Canadian Mounted Police was investigating and that he had informed the Privacy Commissioner of Canada of the breach on Friday.  A CRA spokeswoman said Mr. Treusch wasn't giving interviews.  An RCMP spokeswoman said the matter is with its national division, which is responsible for ""sensitive, high-risk investigations into significant threats to Canada's political, economic and social integrity."" "" She declined to provide more details.  A spokeswoman for National Revenue Minister Kerry-Lynne Findlay, who oversees the CRA, said the ""malicious breach"" was ""contained"" to the six-hour period but decline to provide more details, citing security reasons.  The Conservative government has ""failed to provide Canadians with basic information, like what kind of data the hackers accessed, what their plan is to protect people who filed their taxes online, and how they will ensure that this does not happen again,"" Murray Rankin, a member of the opposition New Democratic Party, said in a statement.  The office of Canada's federal privacy commissioner was closely monitoring technical developments as they emerge to better understand ""the potential privacy implications of Heartbleed,"" a spokeswoman said.  Experts said the nature of the Heartbleed bug means it is very difficult to know when an attack occurs.  Justin Bull, a Toronto-based software developer who believes he was one of the first to inform the CRA that its website was vulnerable, speculated that hackers may have gained access to CRA employees' passwords to get access to taxpayers' personal information.  But cybersecurity expert Satyamoorthy Kabilan said this would have meant many more organizations would have been affected.  He said the hackers may have been able to exploit a vulnerability in communications between the user and the CRA servers.  Investigators likely found such communications had been compromised, such as if it was coming from a different country or from a different IP address.  He said Ottawa acted ""appropriately"" on the matter.  ""One of the biggest issues we have in cybersecurity is people hiding problems in the hope they can fix it,"" said Mr. Kabilan, director of National Security and Strategic Foresight at the Conference Board of Canada, a think tank.  Meanwhile, the CRA was going old-school to inform the people whose information may have been compromised.  It will send them registered letters to inform them of the breach.  OTTAWA--Canadian authorities said Monday that hackers stole the confidential information of about 900 Canadian taxpayers by exploiting the Heartbleed bug, but they were tight-lipped about how and when the Internet security breach occurred.  Canada's national police force was investigating the breach, the government said.  The news came a day after Ottawa reopened access to government websites, and the Canada Revenue Agency, the country's tax authority, said that online tax filing could resume after being suspended for about five days while the agency worked to fix its vulnerability to the software-encryption flaw.  The CRA had made no mention of the breach when it reopened access to electronic tax filing Sunday, even though it said on Monday that it knew of the breach last Friday.  Canada had called the temporary shutdown of the CRA tax-filing service and some other websites a precautionary measure.  Heartbleed, a flaw in encryption software known as Open SSL, can leak key information such as passwords and financial information that could be exploited by hackers.  Monday, the CRA said that some 900 social insurance numbers--akin to U.S. Social Security numbers--were taken from its online tax-filing systems during a six-hour period, an act that it described as ""malicious."" "" The agency didn't specify how or when the breach occurred, and government officials declined to provide details.  Last year, 20 million of the 26.1 million returns received by CRA were filed electronically.  ""We are currently going through the painstaking process of analyzing other fragments of data, some that may relate to businesses, that were also removed,"" the agency's commissioner, Andrew Treusch, said in a statement.  He said that the Royal Canadian Mounted Police was investigating and that he had informed the Privacy Commissioner of Canada of the breach on Friday.  A CRA spokeswoman said Mr. Treusch wasn't giving interviews.  An RCMP spokeswoman said the matter has been assigned to its national division, which is responsible for ""sensitive, high-risk investigations into significant threats to Canada's political, economic and social integrity."" "" She declined to provide more details.  A spokeswoman for National Revenue Minister Kerry-Lynne Findlay, who oversees the CRA, said the ""malicious breach"" was ""contained"" to the six-hour period but declined to provide more details, citing security reasons.  The Conservative government has ""failed to provide Canadians with basic information, such as what kind of data the hackers accessed, what their plan is to protect people who filed their taxes online, and how they will ensure that this does not happen again,"" Murray Rankin, a member of the opposition New Democratic Party, said in a statement.  The office of Canada's federal privacy commissioner is closely monitoring technical developments as they emerge to better understand ""the potential privacy implications of Heartbleed,"" a spokeswoman said.  Experts said the nature of the Heartbleed bug means it is very difficult to know when an attack occurs.  Justin Bull, a Toronto-based software developer who believes he was one of the first to inform the CRA that its website was vulnerable, speculated that hackers may have gained access to CRA employees' passwords to get access to taxpayers' personal information.  But cybersecurity expert Satyamoorthy Kabilan said this would have meant many more organizations would have been affected.  He said the hackers may have been able to exploit a vulnerability in communications between the user and the CRA servers.  Investigators likely found that such communications had been compromised and the attack was coming from a different country or a different IP address, he said.  He added that Ottawa acted ""appropriately"" on the matter.  ""One of the biggest issues we have in cybersecurity is people hiding problems in the hope they can fix it,"" said Mr. Kabilan, director of National Security and Strategic Foresight at the Conference Board of Canada, a think tank.  Meanwhile, the CRA was going old school to inform the people whose information may have been compromised.  It will send them registered letters to inform them of the breach.",2014-04-14,eng,wsj.com,-0.5450980392156863,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/article/BT-CO-20140414-710016.html,3.08
5259,"Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco Routers, Juniper Gear","Cisco Systems and Juniper Networks said that some of their products contain the ""Heartbleed"" bug.  Reuters The encryption bug that has the Internet on high alert also affects the equipment that connects the Web.  Cisco Systems Inc. CSCO +0.13% Cisco Systems Inc. U.S.: Nasdaq $22.68 +0.03 +0.13% April 11, 2014 9:34 am Volume (Delayed 15m) : 0 P/E Ratio 14.80 Market Cap $116.68 Billion Dividend Yield 3.36% Rev. per Employee $637,890 04/11/14 Canada Disables More Online Se... 04/11/14 The Morning Download: Heartble... 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ...  More quote details and news "" CSCO in Your Value Your Change Short position and Juniper Networks Inc., JNPR -0.24% Juniper Networks Inc. U.S.: NYSE $24.79 -0.06 -0.24% April 11, 2014 9:33 am Volume (Delayed 15m) : 0 P/E Ratio 28.56 Market Cap $11.76 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $492,365 04/11/14 Canada Disables More Online Se... 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ... 04/10/14 U.S. Regulators Tell Banks to ...  More quote details and news "" JNPR in Your Value Your Change Short position two of the largest manufacturers of network equipment, said Thursday that some of their products contain the ""Heartbleed"" bug.  That means hackers might be able to capture usernames, passwords and other sensitive information as they move across corporate networks, home networks and the Internet.  Many websites -- including those run by Yahoo Inc., YHOO -3.02% Yahoo!  Inc. U.S.: Nasdaq $32.39 -1.01 -3.02% April 11, 2014 9:34 am Volume (Delayed 15m) : 0 P/E Ratio 25.69 Market Cap $33.71 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $383,392 04/11/14 Canada Disables More Online Se... 04/10/14 Does Japan Have Cultural Barri... 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ...  More quote details and news "" YHOO in Your Value Your Change Short position Amazon.com Inc. and Netflix Inc. -- quickly fixed the hole after it was disclosed Monday.  But Cisco and Juniper said the security flaw affects routers, switches and firewalls often used by businesses.  More on Heartbleed Canada Disables More Online Services Regulators Tell Banks to Plug Security Hole How and Why to Change Passwords Five Questions About Heartbleed These devices likely will be more difficult to fix.  The process involves more steps and businesses are less likely to check the status of network equipment, security experts said.  Bruce Schneier, a cybersecurity researcher and cryptographer, said, ""The upgrade path is going to involve a trash can, a credit card, and a trip to Best Buy."" "" However, products available at retail stores now likely were shipped before the bug was revealed on Monday.  That means they may also contain the defective software, from an encryption code known as OpenSSL.  Companies often use firewalls and virtual private networks to protect their computer systems.  But if the machines that run the firewalls and virtual private networks are affected by the Heartbleed bug, attackers could use them to infiltrate a network, said Matthew Green, an encryption expert at Johns Hopkins University.  WSJD is the Journal's home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  Apple Star Jony Ive to Expand Empire After Heartbleed Bug, a Race to Plug Internet Hole H-P Case Includes Bags of Cash, Watches, Grand Canyon Tours ""It's pretty bad,"" Mr. Green said.  ""Lots and lots of people connect to these things."" "" Mr. Green and others said the bug likely affects some home-networking equipment, such as wireless routers.  Cisco, in a customer bulletin updated Thursday, told clients that dozens of products are ""affected by a vulnerability that could allow an unauthenticated, remote attacker to retrieve"" potentially sensitive information.  In the bulletin, it said 65 products were under investigation and another 16 had been confirmed vulnerable.  Cisco said it would update customers when it has software patches.  Meanwhile, its security researchers offered users software that it said would detect hackers exploiting the bug.  A Cisco spokesman referred a query to the bulletin on its website.  Juniper said the process of updating its equipment might be lengthy.  ""It doesn't sound like a flip the switch sort of thing,"" said Corey Olfert, a Juniper spokesman.  ""I don't know how quickly they can be resolved."" "" The company said it issued patches for some versions of its virtual private network software Tuesday.  It added it is working on patches for other ""affected products"" but didn't elaborate.  To keep prying eyes out, websites and network equipment use encryption to turn sensitive information into a jumble or unreadable text.  Since writing encryption code is complex, developers often use a free, open-source version called OpenSSL.  It's a bare-bones project managed by four European coders.  The Heartbleed bug -- first introduced into OpenSSL two years ago -- allows hackers to grab bits of data from servers and equipment after it has been decrypted.  Write to Danny Yadron at danny.yadron@wsj.com",2014-04-11,eng,wsj.com,-0.584313725490196,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702303873604579493963847851346?mod=pls_whats_news_us_business_f&mg=reno64-wsj&url=http%253A%252F%252Fonline.wsj.com%252Farticle%252FSB10001424052702303873604579493963847851346.html%253Fmod%253Dpls_whats_news_us_business_f,3.08
5260,"Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco Routers, Juniper Gear - Wall Street Journal","The encryption bug that has the Internet on high alert also affects the equipment that connects the Web.  Cisco Systems Inc. CSCO -2.03% Cisco Systems Inc. U.S.: Nasdaq $22.65 -0.47 -2.03% April 10, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 52.52M AFTER HOURS $22.61 -0.04 -0.18% April 10, 2014 6:01 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 1.37M P/E Ratio 14.80 Market Cap $119.10 Billion Dividend Yield 3.36% Rev. per Employee $637,890 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ... 04/09/14 HEARD ON THE STREET: Opening t... 04/08/14 Comcast Dangles Wi-Fi Phone Se...  More quote details and news "" CSCO in Your Value Your Change Short position and Juniper Networks Inc., JNPR -1.82% Juniper Networks Inc. U.S.: NYSE $24.85 -0.46 -1.82% April 10, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 7.61M AFTER HOURS $25.31 +0.46 +1.85% April 10, 2014 4:45 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 37,491 P/E Ratio 28.56 Market Cap $11.98 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $492,365 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ... 04/02/14 Juniper Networks to Cut Global... 03/28/14 What's More Valuable: A Stolen...  More quote details and news "" JNPR in Your Value Your Change Short position two of the largest manufacturers of network equipment, said Thursday that some of their products contain the ""Heartbleed"" bug, meaning hackers might be able to capture user names, passwords and other sensitive information as it moves across corporate networks, home networks and the Internet.  Related Articles Regulators Tell Banks to Plug Security Hole How and Why to Change Passwords Five Questions About Heartbleed Many websites -- including those run by Yahoo Inc., YHOO -4.22% Yahoo!  Inc. U.S.: Nasdaq $33.40 -1.47 -4.22% April 10, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 33.68M AFTER HOURS $33.33 -0.07 -0.21% April 10, 2014 6:04 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 289,554 P/E Ratio 25.69 Market Cap $35.20 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $383,392 04/10/14 Heartbleed Bug Found in Cisco ... 04/10/14 A Middling Outlook for the TV ... 04/10/14 Twitter Proxy Reveals CFO Wind...  More quote details and news "" YHOO in Your Value Your Change Short position Amazon.com Inc. and Netflix Inc. -- quickly fixed the hole after it was disclosed Monday.  But Cisco and Juniper said the security flaw affects routers, switches and firewalls used in businesses and at home.  These devices likely will be more difficult to fix.  The process involves more steps and businesses are less likely to check the status of network equipment, security experts said.  Bruce Schneier, a cybersecurity researcher and cryptographer, said, ""The upgrade path is going to involve a trash can, a credit card, and a trip to Best Buy."" "" But that may not yet be an option: the products available at retail stores now likely were shipped before the bug was revealed on Monday.  So they may also contain the defective software, from an encryption code known as OpenSSL.  Companies often use firewalls and virtual private networks to protect their computer systems.  But if the machines that run the firewalls and virtual private networks are affected by the Heartbleed bug, attackers could use them to infiltrate a network, said Matthew Green, an encryption expert at Johns Hopkins University.  ""It's pretty bad,"" Mr. Green said.  ""Lots and lots of people connect to these things."" "" Mr. Green and others said the bug likely affects some home-networking equipment, such as wireless routers.  In a customer bulletin updated Thursday, Cisco told clients that dozens of products are ""affected by a vulnerability that could allow an unauthenticated, remote attacker to retrieve"" potentially sensitive information.  In the bulletin, it said 65 products were under investigation and another 16 had been confirmed vulnerable.  WSJD is the Journal's home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  Apple Star Jony Ive to Expand Empire After Heartbleed Bug, a Race to Plug Internet Hole H-P Case Includes Bags of Cash, Watches, Grand Canyon Tours Cisco said it would update customers when it has software patches.  In the meantime, its security researchers offered users software that it said would detect hackers exploiting the bug.  A Cisco spokesman referred a query to the bulletin on its website.  Juniper said the process of updating its equipment might be lengthy.  ""It doesn't sound like a flip the switch sort of thing,"" said Corey Olfert, a Juniper spokesman.  ""I don't know how quickly they can be resolved."" "" To keep prying eyes out, websites and network equipment use encryption to turn sensitive information into a jumble or unreadable text.  Since writing encryption code is complex, developers often use a free, open-source version called OpenSSL.  It's a barebones project managed by four European coders.  The Heartbleed bug -- first introduced into OpenSSL two years ago -- allows hackers to grab bits of data from servers and equipment after it has been decrypted.  Write to Danny Yadron at danny.yadron@wsj.com",2014-04-10,eng,wsj.com,-0.4823529411764705,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702303873604579493963847851346?mg=reno64-wsj&url=http%253A%252F%252Fonline.wsj.com%252Farticle%252FSB10001424052702303873604579493963847851346.html,3.08
5261,U.S. Regulators Tell Banks to Plug 'HeartBleed' Security Hole,"FFIEC Says Banks Should Upgrade Software, 'Strongly Consider' Changing Passwords By Ryan TracyAnd Ryan Tracy The Wall Street Journal BiographyRyan Tracy @ryanjtracy Ryan.Tracy@wsj.com Saabira Chaudhuri April 10, 2014 5:24 p.m. ET  WASHINGTON -- U.S. regulators said a vulnerability in banks' computer networks could have exposed sensitive information to hackers, and they instructed both small institutions and Wall Street firms to update their security systems.  Thursday's alert from the Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council said the ""Heartbleed"" encryption bug may have allowed hackers to access the private ""keys"" to banks' servers, allowing them to decrypt and view sensitive information.  WSJD is the Journal's home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  Heartbleed Found in Cisco Routers, Juniper Gear French Workers Turn On 'Out of Office' Dropbox Adds Condoleezza Rice to Board, Rankles Valley Orthodoxy Apple Star Jony Ive to Expand Empire  The Federal Deposit Insurance Corp., one of the members of the council, said it wasn't aware of attackers exploiting the Heartbleed flaw that became public in recent days but said banks should assume that their security systems may have been breached. Regulators said hackers may have accessed sensitive information without the banks' knowledge.  Earlier this week, security researchers reported the vulnerability, dubbed Heartbleed, in a widely used encryption device known as OpenSSL. Banks and many other companies use the code to protect passwords, emails and other sensitive data from eavesdropping.  The security alert highlighted the growing number of cyberthreats faced by banks, which have had to bolster their defenses as more financial transactions take place online. Last week, regulators issued a separate alert warning against attacks on automated teller machines that could allow thieves to make huge unauthorized cash withdrawals.  The warning came as two of the largest manufacturers of network equipment, Cisco Systems Inc. and Juniper Networks Inc., said Thursday that some of their products contain the Heartbleed bug, giving hackers the ability to capture usernames, passwords and other sensitive information as it travels across the Internet.  Many websites -- including those run by Yahoo Inc., Amazon.com Inc. and Netflix Inc. -- quickly fixed the hole after it was disclosed Monday.  But Cisco and Juniper said the security flaw affects routers, switches and firewalls used in businesses and at home. Several of the nation's biggest banks said they were reviewing their systems in the wake of the bug but hadn't experienced any breach.  "" Bank of America has experienced no issues as a result of the Heartbleed Bug, and has determined our sites are not vulnerable,"" a Bank of America Corp. spokesman said.  A Citigroup Inc. spokeswoman said the bank's ""initial assessment indicates it has not impacted our retail banking or credit card websites, and we are taking appropriate steps to safeguard all of our websites.""  A Wells Fargo & Co. spokeswoman said the ""vulnerabilities associated with Heartbleed have had no impact on the safety of banking online with Wells Fargo.""  The regulatory alert didn't say bank customers' passwords may have been compromised and focused more on internal systems and passwords used by employees. Several banks said they generally recommend that website users frequently change their passwords and avoid using the same password with multiple accounts.  ""We believe that financial institutions and their technology-service providers have been managing system updates to mitigate potential vulnerabilities in an effective manner,"" Doreen Eberley, director of the FDIC's division of risk management supervision, said.  Ms. Eberley's statement was part of a separate FDIC news release issued earlier Thursday listing resources to help banks identify and stop attacks, including tips and alerts distributed by the Department of Homeland Security and other agencies.  The Heartbleed vulnerability has existed since December 2011, the regulators said. Many banks also rely on third parties to provide online banking services or secure their systems, and regulators said banks should ensure their contractors make system upgrades to plug any holes.  Write to Ryan Tracy at ryan.tracy@wsj.com",2014-04-10,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-regulators-tell-banks-to-plug-heartbleed-security-hole-1397164986,3.08
5262,Data Breach Suit Against Target Also Names Security Firm Trustwave,"By Danny Yadron Danny Yadron The Wall Street Journal BiographyDanny Yadron @dannyyadron Danny.Yadron@wsj.com March 27, 2014 8:30 p.m. ET  A lawsuit stemming from the security breach at Target Corp. TGT 1.36% spotlights a private Chicago firm that does most of the payment-security checks in the U.S., including the retail giant's.  In a lawsuit filed in U.S. district court in Chicago this week, two U.S. banks say that Trustwave Holdings Inc. is liable for the theft of 40 million credit- and debit-card numbers from Target last year.  The banks, Trustmark National Bank and Green Bank N.A., had to reimburse their card customers who experienced fraudulent charges after shopping at Target, which along with Trustwave was named as a defendant in the suit.  A Trustwave spokeswoman declined to comment. Target declined to comment. The banks' attorneys declined to comment beyond court filings.  WSJD is the Journal's home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  Amazon Plans Free Streaming Media Service Facebook Creates Team to 'Beam' Internet Everywhere on Earth Hands-On: Microsoft Office for iPad Puts Fingers First After Twitter Ban, Turkey's Netizens Find YouTube Blockage a Breeze Cash Piles, Paranoia Fuel Tech Giants' Buying Binge  The lawsuit raises questions about Trustwave's position in the credit-card industry. To avoid fines from card issuers, retailers often hire Trustwave to conduct security audits of their systems. J.P. Morgan Chase & Co. and PNC Financial Services GroupInc. advertise discounted assessments from Trustwave on their websites. American Express Co. uses the firm to run its security compliance program.  In the complaint, lawyers for the banks claim Trustwave's shortcomings ""allowed hackers to cause the data breach."" The complaint states: ""Trustwave failed to timely discover and report the data breach to Target or the public.""  Such lawsuits from banks are common after a hack. But the banks typically sue the company that was hacked, not a company hired to provide security services.  On its website, Trustwave said it has done more payment-card security audits than all other security companies combined. But Trustwave also offers security-consulting and network-monitoring services for retailers. In the suit, the banks claim Target hired Trustwave ""to protect and monitor Target's computer systems.""  Some in the security industry question whether it is appropriate for companies that assess security practices to also sell remedies.  ""It's a conflict of interest telling the company, 'You've got 10 things wrong and we can help you fix those 10 things,'"" said Avivah Litan, a senior analyst at Gartner. ""There's no objectivity.""  Tom Arnold, a principal at PSC, another company that conducts security audits of retailers, said his firm also offers consulting services. But it has a rule: It will only do one or the other for a client.  Last September, Trustwave told Target that its payment-security system met industry guidelines, called PCI security standards, according to the lawsuit. Target executives have cited this assessment as evidence they took security seriously.  Credit-card companies created PCI standards in 2004 as minimum security steps for companies that handle payment cards. These standards dictate how companies store and process credit- and debit-card data, requiring regular monitoring and testing of networks, for example. If retailers don't adhere to them, the card issuers can fine them.  Trustwave executives have said the credit-card issuers' guidelines won't by themselves prevent all hacks. And because the banks had no contractual relationship with Trustwave, it is unclear they have legal standing to sue the security company, several attorneys and analysts said.  Brian Finch, a partner at Dickstein Shapiro LLP, who works with utilities, sports teams and the high-tech sector on cybersecurity, said it is unfair to blame the security company when a client is hacked. ""That's a terrible position any time one of your customers suffers a problem, you'll be fingered,"" Mr. Finch said.  In 2012, a South Carolina woman filed a purported class-action lawsuit against the company after hackers stole tax-return data from the South Carolina Department of Revenue. Like Target, the state used Trustwave for security services.  This month, a federal judge dismissed that case, ruling the woman had no grounds to sue.  Write to Danny Yadron at danny.yadron@wsj.com",2014-03-28,eng,wsj.com, ,false,news,https://www.wsj.com/articles/data-breach-suit-against-target-also-names-security-firm-trustwave-1395966582,3.08
5263,Hackers Tailored Malware to Retailers,"Government investigators looking into the cyberattacks on Neiman Marcus Group and Target Corp. TGT -0.09% Target Corp. U.S.: NYSE $55.07 -0.05 -0.09% Feb. 5, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 8.11M AFTER HOURS $55.34 +0.27 +0.49% Feb. 5, 2014 7:59 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 76,339 P/E Ratio 14.65 Market Cap $34.84 Billion Dividend Yield 3.12% Rev. per Employee $204,452 02/05/14 Hackers Tailored Malware to Re... 02/04/14 Target Data Breach Went on Lon... 02/04/14 Retailers, Banks Point Fingers...  More quote details and news "" TGT in Your Value Your Change Short position believe the malicious software used in the heists was specifically tailored to exploit vulnerabilities in each retailer's checkout systems.  The malware behind the two attacks is different, William Noonan, a top official in the Secret Service's cyber operations division, said Wednesday at a House Energy and Commerce subcommittee hearing in Washington.  The Secret Service investigations into both breaches are ongoing, but the details of the software make clear retailers face an organized and persistent threat from hackers hoping to steal credit-card data.  To properly modify the software, hackers would have to develop intimate knowledge of the system and the security protocols in place.  In the case of Neiman Marcus, the hackers appeared to have entered their system sometime in early 2013 to perform reconnaissance before implanting the malware that would carry out an attack that exposed data from up to 1.1 million cards, the retailer said.  The malware that compromised 40 million credit and debit cards at Target also appears to have been tailored to that retailer.  The hackers that attacked the discounter appear to have gotten in by using credentials stolen from a heating and air-conditioning contractor, a person familiar with the matter said.  Target previously said the attackers gained entry with credentials stolen from a vendor, but didn't say which kind.  On Wednesday, the company declined to comment.  ""This continues to be a very active and ongoing investigation,"" Target spokeswoman Molly Snyder said.  Cybersecurity blogger Brian Krebs reported the identity of the vendor earlier Wednesday, saying it was Fazio Mechanical Services, a Pennsylvania-based provider of heating and air-conditioning systems.  Fazio didn't respond to a request for comment.  While Target and Neiman Marcus suffered the most visible recent attacks, the Federal Bureau of Investigation and the Secret Service have warned that other retailers have been hit as well.  Phillip Smith, a former Secret Service agent now with cybersecurity firm Trustwave Holdings, said a number of companies have been infiltrated and have software in their systems doing reconnaissance.  The reconnaissance involves getting into the point-of-sale system, watching how it processes transactions and figuring out how to best steal the data.  The practice is leading to more sophisticated customization, Mr. Smith said.  ""It's not an off-the-shelf type of malware,"" said Mr. Noonan, the Secret Service official.  ""The criminals are modifying and molding specific types of malware."" "" Mr. Noonan described the gangs behind the attacks as being loosely affiliated but highly coordinated.  He likened them to the heist movie ""Ocean's Eleven."" "" The groups have specialists in areas of infiltrating systems, designing malware, mapping networks and selling stolen data, and they employ them to work on different phases of the attack, he said.  The attacks on Target and Neiman Marcus appear to be unrelated, based on tactics and malware used, according to people familiar with the matter.  Write to Paul Ziobro at Paul.Ziobro@wsj.com and Danny Yadron at danny.yadron@wsj.com",2014-02-06,eng,wsj.com,-0.4274509803921569,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702304450904579365253770776432,3.08
5264,Target Breach Began With Contractor's Electronic Billing Link,"The hackers that carried out the massive data breach at Target Corp. TGT +1.43% Target Corp. U.S.: NYSE $55.86 +0.79 +1.43% Feb. 6, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 8.87M AFTER HOURS $55.75 -0.11 -0.20% Feb. 6, 2014 7:04 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 49,662 P/E Ratio 14.86 Market Cap $34.81 Billion Dividend Yield 3.08% Rev. per Employee $204,452 02/06/14 Target Breach Began With Contr... 02/05/14 Hackers Tailored Malware to Re... 02/04/14 Target Data Breach Went on Lon...  More quote details and news "" TGT in Your Value Your Change Short position appear to have gained access via a refrigeration contractor in Pittsburgh that connected to the retailer's systems to do electronic billing.  Fazio Mechanical Services Inc., a privately held company with about 125 employees, said Thursday it was ""a victim of a sophisticated cyber attack operation"" and was cooperating with investigators at the Secret Service.  Full Target Coverage Target Breach Traced to Heating Contractor Target Hackers Used Stolen Vendor Credentials Version of Target Malware Linked to Young Russian Card-Theft Software Grew in Internet's Dark Alleys Target Now Says 70 Million People Hit in Data Breach The details in a statement by the company's owner, Ross Fazio, provide new clues to how hackers infiltrated Target's computer system and eventually stole the data of 40 million credit- and debit-card numbers during a security breach that lasted from Nov. 27 to Dec. 18.  The thieves also stole personal data like email addresses and phone numbers of 70 million customers.  Fazio Mechanical began working with Target in 2006 installing and maintaining refrigerator systems in stores as the discounter expanded its fresh food offerings.  Through that relationship, the contractor was linked remotely to Target's computer systems for ""electronic billing, contract submission and project management,"" Mr. Fazio said in the statement.  Secret Service agents visited Fazio Mechanical's offices earlier in the week, Fazio spokesman Dick Roberts said Thursday.  The contractor is now helping investigators from the Secret Service and Target figure out how the hackers were able to access Fazio's network.  The connection between the two is another reminder of the risks faced by large corporations when they grant contractors access to their large, interconnected computer systems.  Hackers commonly go after low-level victims to get credentials to access a bigger company's network.  Then they move through the system until they find a company's crown jewels -- in this case credit and debit card numbers.  Introducing WSJD, the Journal's new home for tech news, analysis and product reviews.  LinkedIn's Sales Growth Disappoints Wall Street Hard Times for a Bitcoin Evangelist Inside the Microsoft CEO Search News App Showdown The Business Card Cannot Be Killed Target last week said the hackers infiltrated its system using stolen vendor credentials, without providing further detail.  Cybersecurity blogger Brian Krebs on Wednesday reported that Fazio Mechanical was the vendor that was infiltrated.  A Target spokeswoman declined to comment Thursday.  Fazio Mechanical operates in five states helping to install and maintain refrigerators for supermarket chains and other companies.  Its clients include BJ's Wholesale Club Inc., Costco Wholesale Corp. COST +3.39% Costco Wholesale Corp. U.S.: Nasdaq $114.26 +3.75 +3.39% Feb. 6, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 3.72M AFTER HOURS $114.35 +0.09 +0.08% Feb. 6, 2014 7:58 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 31,865 P/E Ratio 24.36 Market Cap $48.59 Billion Dividend Yield 1.09% Rev. per Employee $578,576 02/06/14 January Retail Sales Stung by ... 01/24/14 Wal-Mart to Lay Off 2,300 Sam'... 12/11/13 Costco's Profit Up; Revenue Le...  More quote details and news "" COST in Your Value Your Change Short position , Supervalu Inc., SVU +2.36% SUPERVALU Inc. U.S.: NYSE $5.65 +0.13 +2.36% Feb. 6, 2014 4:00 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 2.64M AFTER HOURS $5.65 0.00 0.00% Feb. 6, 2014 4:22 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 3,358 P/E Ratio N/A Market Cap $1.43 Billion Dividend Yield N/A Rev. per Employee $485,943 01/13/14 Supervalu Chairman Robert Mill... 01/09/14 Supervalu Profit Rises on Save... 01/08/14 AHEAD OF THE TAPE: After Rebou...  More quote details and news "" SVU in Your Value Your Change Short position Trader Joe's and Wal-Mart Stores Inc. WMT -0.07% Wal-Mart Stores Inc. U.S.: NYSE $72.82 -0.05 -0.07% Feb. 6, 2014 4:03 pm Volume (Delayed 15m) : 7.14M AFTER HOURS $72.82 0.00 0.00% Feb. 6, 2014 7:51 pm Volume (Delayed 15m): 217,270 P/E Ratio 13.95 Market Cap $235.79 Billion Dividend Yield 2.58% Rev. per Employee $215,790 02/06/14 January Retail Sales Stung by ... 02/04/14 Wal-Mart to Spend C$500 Millio... 02/04/14 What WSJ Is Reading Tuesday More quote details and news "" WMT in Your Value Your Change Short position Target was the only customer to which Fazio Mechanical had remote access, and no other customer was affected in the breach, Mr. Fazio said in the statement.  Fazio lists Target as a client on its website, but it is unclear how the hackers would have thought to look for the company or learned Fazio had access to Target's systems.  A search on database service Factiva only shows one mention of the two companies together: A November 2012 newspaper listing of construction permits, including two for renovation and expansion of the grocery departments at two Target stores in projects conducted by Fazio costing $325,315 and $319,810.  Federal investigators are also looking into a similar data breach at the luxury retailer Neiman Marcus Group, where over the course of several months hackers may have stolen data from as many as 1.1 million payment cards.  At congressional hearings earlier this week, Neiman Chief Information Officer Michael Kingston said the company hasn't determined how hackers entered their system to plant the malicious software.  --Danny Yadron contributed to this article.  Write to Paul Ziobro at Paul.Ziobro@wsj.com",2014-02-06,eng,wsj.com,-0.4901960784313726,false,news,http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702304450904579367391844060778,3.85
